he\u000awire, a mass, and the clip. There is an overlying skin ellipse (3.7 x REMOVED_DATE cm),\u000ain which no definitive scar can be identified. A firm white mass (1.1 x REMOVED_DATE x\u000a0.7 cm) is present REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior\u000amargin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior margin, and >2 cm from the inferior, medial\u000aand lateral margins. There is an area of fibrosis adjacent to the mass that\u000afocally extends to the posterior margin. The remainder of the breast parenchyma\u000ais composed of 100% adipose tissue. The mass is entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: mass to closest posterior margin, 2 frags, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: mass to closest anterior and superior margins, 1 frag, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: additional superior and anterior margins, 2 frags, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: closest inferior margin with area of fibrosis, 2 frags, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: closest medial margin, 2 frags, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: closest lateral margin, 2 frags, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: skin, 2 frags, RSS\u000aBGL/jp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Briana Gleason M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by William R Welch M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
