VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Dick Guang-Iong Hwang, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A 1 CM IRREGULAR MASS:\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia.\u000aNote: By report, the targeted lesion was not present in this specimen,\u000abut was present in the subsequent excision (see below).\u000aNo carcinoma is seen after complete microscopic examination of the\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aLEFT BREAST, SUPERIOR SHAVE MARGIN:\u000aFibroadenoma (0.4 cm).\u000aSclerosing adenosis with calcifications.\u000aNote: The patient had a palpable mass that correlated with a 1 cm\u000airregular mass by ultrasound. A core needle biopsy showed only normal\u000atissue (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). A clip was placed at the biopsy site.\u000aBy report, an ultrasound examination of the current specimen revealed\u000athe targeted mass. A gross mass was not identified. All tissue is examined\u000amicroscopically. There is a small fibroadenoma that does not appear to\u000acorrelate with the imaging findings. The specimen consists of approximately\u000a2 cm of dense tissue that may have simulated a mass in a fatty breast. The\u000aprior core needle biopsy site and the clip are not present in the specimen.\u000aRadiologic correlation will be required to determine if the targeted mass is\u000apresent in this specimen.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 55 year old post-menopausal female with left breast mass REMOVED_DATE  US\u000arevealed heterogenous/complex solid nodule at 2 o'clock - core biopsy REMOVED_DATE \u000arevealed normal breast tissue and wire localization was recommended as results\u000awere discordant.\u000aOperation: Left breast wire localized biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: None given.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast tissue long - lateral, short - superior, whip stitch on deep\u000amargin (to mammo).\u000aB/2. Superior shave margin whip stitch on new margin (to mammo).\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000a#1. No mass - fat only and wire.\u000a#2. Mass in smaller specimen. OR was phoned.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, "#1. Left breast tissue, short superior, long lateral, whipstitch on\u000adeep margin", consists of a portion of fibrofatty tissue (5.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm),\u000awith a long suture designating lateral, a short suture designating superior,\u000aand a whipstitch designating the deep margin. Also received within the same\u000acontainer is an unattached silver-colored metallic wire, consistent with a\u000aneedle-localization wire. The specimen radiograph does not reveal any lesions.\u000aThe specimen is inked per pathology protocol as anterior blue, posterior black,\u000asuperior red, inferior green, medial orange, and lateral yellow, and is\u000aserially sectioned to reveal 95% yellow lobulated adipose tissue, and 5% white\u000afibrous tissue, with no discrete masses or lesions identified. The specimen is\u000asequentially submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin, radially, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, from superior to inferior, 1 frag each,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential bisected cross section, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential bisected cross section, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential bisected cross section, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential bisected cross section, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential bisected cross section, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential bisected cross section, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential bisected cross section, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, radially, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID multi frags, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID 2 frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Superior shave margin, stitch on new margin", consists of a\u000aportion of fibrofatty tissue (3.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), with a suture on one side,\u000adesignating the new margin. This new margin is inked blue, and the opposite\u000amargin is inked black, and the specimen is serially sectioned to reveal 95%\u000adense white fibrous tissue and 5% yellow lobulated adipose tissue, with no\u000adiscrete masses or lesions identified. The specimen radiograph reveals dense\u000atissue, without a specific lesion. The fibrous tissue extends to both the\u000ablue inked new margin and opposite black inked margin. The specimen is\u000asequentially submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 unoriented end, radially, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 2 frags each; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID:\u000a1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Opposite unoriented end, radially, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000acp/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a11/9/2007\u000a[report_end]
