VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ROGER L CHRISTIAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Elgida Radoncipi, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Jane E. Brock, M.B., B.S., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT BREAST, EXCISION WIRE LOCALIZATION FOR CALCIFICATIONS AND A MASS:\u000aINVASIVE LOBULAR CARCINOMA\u000aCARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aINVASIVE CARCINOMA\u000aSize - REMOVED_DATE cm, measured on the glass slide\u000asee NOTE\u000aHistologic type - Lobular\u000aHistologic grade (SBR) - II (Moderate) (6 to 7 points)\u000aTubules - < 10% (3 points)\u000aNuclear grade - Intermediate (2 points)\u000aMitotic counts - 1 point\u000aLVI - Absent\u000aFinal Margins (invasive)\u000aNegative (> REMOVED_DATE cm) - Medial, superior, inferior,\u000aanterior, posterior\u000aVery close(<0.1 cm from margin)- Lateral (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aCARCINOMA IN SITU - with mixed features\u000aTypes - Solid, cribriform, micropapillary\u000aNuclear grade - Low & intermediate\u000aCalcifications in CIS - Present (see NOTE)\u000aNecrosis in CIS - Present (single cell necrosis)\u000aExtent of CIS\u000aNumber of blocks/total - 16:16 blocks (see NOTE)\u000aMargins (CIS)\u000aNegative (> REMOVED_DATE cm) - Superior\u000aClose (0.1 to REMOVED_DATE cm) - Medial (0.2 cm, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aPosterior (0.2 cm, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aVery close(<0.1 cm from margin)- Lateral (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aAnterior (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aInferior/lateral junction (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, All)\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU - Present\u000aOTHER FINDINGS\u000aPseudoangiomatous stromal hyperplasia\u000aRuptured cysts with extensive stromal inflammation and stromal hemosiderin\u000adeposition\u000aIntraductal papillomas with sclerosis\u000aColumnar cell change with calcifications\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER, PR, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) - Pending\u000aAJCC Classification (6th edition): REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDb\u000aNOTE:\u000aInvasive carcinoma is present on 3 of 16 slides as a REMOVED_DATE cm focus on each\u000aslide, close to lateral margin (the involved slides likely represent the same\u000afocus of invasive carcinoma; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). The carcinoma in situ shows a\u000aspectrum of morphology ranging from classic low nuclear grade LCIS to CIS of\u000aintermediate grade with solid and cribriform cohesive architecture.\u000aIntermediate grade CIS has various patterns; discohesive with single cell\u000anecrosis, CIS with micropapillary and cribriform features (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) and CIS\u000ainvolving intraductal papillomas which due to papillary architecture present\u000amakes it difficult to distinguish LCIS from DCIS. CIS is associated with\u000acalcifications but it is likely some of the calcifications in neoplastic ducts\u000awere preexisting in the breast epithelium. For the purpose of margin\u000aevaluation, margins have been reported for all but the classic low nuclear\u000agrade LCIS present.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Abnormal mammo; LT breast\u000aOperation: Wire directed excision, abnormal LT breast\u000aOperative Findings: Abnormal area LT breast\u000aClinical Diagnosis: See op note\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast tissue, long suture lateral, short suture superior\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number\u000aand "left breast tissue, long suture lateral, short suture superior", consists\u000aof an irregular pink/tan to tan/yellow lobulated portion of fibroadipose breast\u000atissue (4.0 cm anterior to posterior, REMOVED_DATE cm superior to inferior, and REMOVED_DATE cm\u000amedial to lateral) with a short suture designating the superior margin, and a\u000along suture designating the lateral margins. There is a needle-localization\u000awire which enters through the anterior superior aspect of the specimen and\u000aexits through the posterior lateral aspect of the specimen. The specimen is\u000ainked per pathology protocol (superior red, inferior green, medial orange,\u000alateral yellow, anterior blue and posterior black) and is serially sectioned\u000afrom anterior to posterior. The cut surface is tan/yellow and homogeneous with\u000afocal areas of dense white fibrous tissue constituting approximately 90% of\u000abreast parenchyma. No discrete lesions are grossly identified. There are no\u000aareas of calcification or surgical clips designated per the specimen\u000aradiograph. The specimen is submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections anterior to posterior, 1 frag each,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Posterior margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aSEK/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aMonday REMOVED_MONTH 09, 2009 at 11:32:49AM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM:\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma and CIS (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not\u000ayet been determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but\u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a< 30% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>30% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aAddendum #1 by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aMonday REMOVED_MONTH 09, 2009 at 05:54:39PM\u000a[report_end]
