VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aCRUM, CHRISTOPHER, PAUL, M.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by CRUM,CHRISTOPHER PAUL,M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "LEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED, EXCISIONAL BIOPSY FOR\u000aCALCIFICATIONS AND A CLIP":\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia with Pagetoid spread (block 2).\u000aAtypical epithelial proliferation with secretory changes and\u000acalcifications. (See note).\u000aNote: The atypical proliferation is less prominent than that seen in the\u000aprior core needle biopsy.\u000aDiagnostic features of malignancy are not seen.\u000aThe breast slides were reviewed by Dr. Susan Lester who concurs.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "ENDOCERVICAL CURETTINGS":\u000aSquamous metaplasia.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "ENDOMETRIAL CURETTINGS":\u000aEndometrial Intraepithelial Neoplasia (atypical endometrial hyperplasia)\u000awith stromal breakdown, see comment.\u000aComment: Follow-up endometrial sampling is advised.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Postmenopausal bleeding.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Postmenopausal bleeding - breast mass.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: 1. Left needle localization at 1:00 - to mammo (short\u000aanterior and long stitch superior)\u000a2. ECC\u000a3. EMC\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by BROCK,JANE E.,M.B.,B.S.,PH.D.\u000aRadiology Reports:\u000aClip and calcifications in specimen. Dr. E. Frost.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh in three parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart one, "left breast biopsy short anterior, long superior", consists of a\u000awire localized breast lumpectomy specimen (0.8 cm from superior to inferior,\u000a2.5 cm from anterior to superior, 4 cm from medial to lateral), with a long\u000astitch designating superior and a short stitch designating anterior. The\u000aspecimen is received with a radiograph which demonstrates a wire, clip and\u000aan ill-defined lesion with possible areas of microcalcification. Sectioning\u000areveals approximately 90% firm white fibrous tissue, with no obvious lesions.\u000aThe specimen is entirely submitted.\u000aThe specimen is inked as follows: superior red, inferior green, anterior blue,\u000aposterior black, medial orange, lateral yellow.\u000aMicro sections:\u000aMicro 1: medial margin, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  tissue submitted medial to lateral including superior inferior\u000aanterior and posterior margins, 5 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 4: lateral margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aPart two, "ECC", consists of hemorrhagic tan/pink tissue (0.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in\u000aaggregate). The specimen is entirely submitted.\u000aMicro 5: ECC, ESS.\u000aPart three, "EMC", consists of hemorrhagic tan soft tissue (2 x 2 x REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aThe specimen is entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  EMC, multiple frags, ESS.\u000aReports to: REILLY, RAYMOND, JOHN, M.B.B.C.H.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: ENDOCERVICAL CURETTINGS (ECC) OR BIOPSY\u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: ENDOMETRIAL CURETTINGS (EMC) OR BIOPSY\u000a[report_end]
