VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aLESTER, SUSAN, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by LESTER,SUSAN,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, EXCISION, WIRE LOCALIZATION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aSEVERELY ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA BORDERING ON DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN\u000aSITU, is present in three of 13 blocks (2, 4, and 6; levels examined).\u000aExtensive ADH lining mucin-filled cysts is present in ten of thirteen\u000a(10:13) blocks.\u000aADH is present < REMOVED_DATE cm from multiple inked margins (not oriented).\u000aNote: Multiple small foci (each < REMOVED_DATE cm) of ADH closely approach a\u000adiagnosis of DCIS. However, given the small size of each focus, a\u000adiagnosis of ADH is favored. Excision is recommended due to the\u000adifficulty in distinguishing ADH from small foci of DCIS in this specimen\u000aand the histologic similarity between the areas approaching a diagnosis of\u000aDCIS and the ADH.\u000aThere are areas of extravesated mucin in stroma associated with\u000amucin-filled cysts. Additional levels do not reveal epithelial cells\u000awithin the mucin. Therefore, no invasive carcinoma is seen.\u000aThis case was reviewed at the Staff Pathology Conference.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Microcalcifications in right breast.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: 1. Tissue from right breast.\u000aMammography: Calcifications are in the specimen. ... Dr. Darrell Smith\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by BUNDOCK,ELIZABETH A.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh in one part, labeled with the patient's name and\u000aunit number, and consists of one (1) unoriented portion of fibroadipose tissue\u000a(4.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with areas of firm tan-white fibrotic tissue\u000a(approximately one-third of the specimen). The localization wire is received\u000aunattached to mass and free floating in specimen container. The specimen is\u000areceived with mammogram, demonstrating calcifications in the specimen.\u000aThe specimen is sectioned to reveal poorly defined areas of fibrosis with one\u000a(1) area (1.9 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which may contain calcifications based on\u000apalpation. The specimen is submitted entirely as follows.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  breast tissue containing possible calcifications, mult frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  remainder of breast tissue, 17 frags, ESS.\u000aeab\u000aReports to: CHRISTIAN, ROGER, L. M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
