VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY(IES)\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aRICHARDSON, ANDREA, L., M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by RICHARDSON,ANDREA L.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aLEFT BREAST, LOWER INNER, 11 G STEREOTACTIC CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY FOR\u000aCALCIFICATIONS:\u000aSEVERELY ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA in multiple cores, focally bordering\u000aon DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU.\u000aColumnar alteration of lobules with prominent snouts and secretions WITH\u000aATYPIA.\u000aCalcifications associated with atypical ductal hyperplasia.\u000aNOTE: Atypical ductal hyperplasia is best evaluated on surgical excision.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, UPPER OUTER, 11 G STEREOTACTIC CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY FOR\u000aCALCIFICATIONS:\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU is present.\u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA is present, associated with calcifications,\u000aarising in columnar alteration of lobules with prominent snouts and\u000asecretions.\u000aNOTE: Atypical ductal hyperplasia is best evaluated on surgical\u000aexcision.\u000aNOTE: Several foci of small atypical glands are present on both biopsies,\u000ahowever, immunohistochemistry for p63 (on blocks 2, 3 and 4) shows basal cells\u000aaround these glands confirming the benign nature.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Microcalcifications both breasts.\u000aLeft - lower inner.\u000aRight - upper outer.\u000aOperation: 11 G stereotactic.\u000aOperative findings: Left - calcification in cores.\u000aRight - calcification in cores.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: ? benign or DCIS.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: 1. Left breast tissue in blue cassette; 1 = with ca; B =\u000ano ca.\u000a2. Right breast tissue in white cassettes; A = with ca,\u000aB = no ca.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by PREISINGER,ELIZABETH ANN,M.D.\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin in two parts, each labeled with the\u000apatient's name and unit number.\u000aPart one, labeled "Left breast tissue", consists of two (2) light blue\u000acassettes. One cassette is labeled "A-Ca++" and contains multiple, soft,\u000afragmented, yellow to white tissue cores (1.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate).\u000aThe other blue cassette is labeled "B" and contains similar tissue fragments\u000a(1.6 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate).\u000aMicro 1: "A", multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 2: "B", multi frags, ESS.\u000aPart two, labeled "right breast tissue" consists of two (2) white cassettes.\u000aOne cassette is labeled "A Ca++" and contains multiple, soft, fragmented,\u000ayellow to white tissue cores (1.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate). The other\u000awhite cassette is labeled "B" and contains similar tissue fragments (1.7 x REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate).\u000aMicro 3: "A", multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 4: "B", multi frags, ESS.\u000akks\u000aReports to: TWOHIG, LORRAINE, BERNADETTE, MB\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY(IES)\u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY(IES)\u000a[report_end]
