VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST MASTECTOMY - PARTIAL OR SIMPLE\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aLESTER, SUSAN, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by LESTER,SUSAN,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aLEFT BREAST, RE-EXCISION, WIRE LOCALIZED FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aSeverely atypical ductal hyperplasia is present as small foci\u000a(0.2-0.3 cm), in 3 of 8 blocks (blocks 1, 4, and 5; levels examined)\u000a(see note).\u000aCalcifications are present in the atypical ductal hyperplasia.\u000aThe atypical ductal hyperplasia is <0.1 cm from the inferior and lateral\u000amargins.\u000aNOTE: The atypical cell population approaches a diagnosis of low grade\u000aductal carcinoma in situ but does not completely fill the involved\u000aspaces.\u000a(Clinical history: 48 year old woman with a history of severely atypical\u000aductal hyperplasia/borderline ductal carcinoma in situ diagnosed by Dr. Stuart\u000aSchnitt on a prior biopsy specimen.)\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 48 year old premenopausal female not on hormones s/p REMOVED_DATE  left\u000abreast wire localized excisional biopsy with calcifications at another facility\u000awith pathology revealing ADH vs DCIS. REMOVED_DATE  post biopsy mammogram reveals 2\u000afoci of residual calcifications.\u000aOperation: Left wire localized partial mastectomy\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal mammogram - calcifications and biopsy cavity\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: Left breast wire localized partial mastectomy\u000aRADIOLOGY: Calcifications in specimen. E.P. Frost\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by ABBOTT,DEREK W,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name and unit number\u000aonly, and consists of a single fragment of breast tissue (3.9 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aThe specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior = black; anterior = blue; superior = red; inferior = green; lateral =\u000ayellow; medial = orange. The specimen is received with a wire in it. The\u000aspecimen is serially sectioned to reveal a hemorrhagic area (0.4 cm in\u000adiameter) lying REMOVED_DATE cm from the deep margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior margin,\u000a1.2 cm from the superior margin and REMOVED_DATE cm from the superficial margin. The\u000aspecimen is entirely submitted for histologic analysis.\u000aThe specimen radiograph is examined and reveals a wire and a small cluster of\u000acalcifications.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  breast tissue, 1 frag each cassette, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  breast tissue, 2 frags each cassette, ESS\u000aAlso received are the following pathology reports:\u000aNewton-Wellesley Hospital, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID:3219, REMOVED_DATE  left breast (5 to 6 o'clock),\u000aneedle biopsies: "Atypical ductal hyperplasia with microcalcifications".\u000aBeth Israel Deaconess Hospital, REMOVED_DATE  REMOVED_DATE  consult slides from\u000aNewton-Wellesley Hospital:\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID:3219: Atypical ductal hyperplasia with associated calcifications.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Severely atypical ductal hyperplasia/borderline ductal\u000acarcinoma in situ, present on 8 of 11 slides and extending to inked\u000amargin. Calcifications present in association with atypical ductal\u000alesion.\u000adwa\u000aReports to: KAELIN, CAROLYN, MARY M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST MASTECTOMY - PARTIAL OR SIMPLE\u000a[report_end]
