VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: DIANE C LOCKHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Martin C Chang, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: None\u000aResident\u000aMARTIN C CHANG, M.D., PH.D.\u000aPathologist\u000aMARISA ROSE NUCCI, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED RE-EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia with pagetoid spread.\u000aColumnar cell change with atypia and associated microcalcifications.\u000aAbundant calcifications associated with benign epithelium.\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia, cysts, and apocrine metaplasia.\u000aForeign body giant cell reaction consistent with prior biopsy site.\u000aNOTE: This case was reviewed by Dr. D. Dillon, breast pathology, who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 52 year old woman s/p right needle loc breast bx REMOVED_DATE  read as ADH,\u000aALH. Screening mammo revealed persistent microcalcs with question in increase\u000ain number.\u000aOperation: Right needle loc breast excision\u000aOperative Findings: None given\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right abnormal mammogram\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aRight breast tissue (to mammo)\u000aRADIOLOGY: Extensive calcifications b/w wire brackets, prior bx showed ADH,\u000as/p excisional bx showed ADH, now re-excision of all ca++.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient?s name, unit number,\u000aand ?#1. right breast needle loc?, and consists of a single fragment of\u000afibroadipose tissue measuring REMOVED_DATE (S-I) x REMOVED_DATE (A-B) x REMOVED_DATE (M-L). The specimen\u000ais inked presumably per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. Contained within the specimen are two localization wires\u000acorresponding to the border of a region of numerous microcalcifications on the\u000aaccompanying specimen radiographs. The specimen is composed 80% of adipose\u000atissue and 20% of fibrous tissue with no discrete mass lesions. It is entirely\u000asubmitted from superior to inferior in serial sections.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: superior edge, mult frags, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: right breast tissue in serial sections from superior to\u000ainferior, mult frags, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: inferior edge, mult frags, ESS\u000aMcc/jl\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aMicroscopic/Diagnostic Dictation by Martin C Chang M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Marisa R Nucci M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
