VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY(IES)\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aBOROWSKY, ALEXANDER, D., M.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by BOROWSKY,ALEXANDER D.,M.D.\u000aLEFT BREAST, 11G STEREOTACTIC CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aApocrine hyperplasia with focal cribriform architecture suspicious for\u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA.\u000aApocrine cysts with associated inflammation and cholesterol clefts,\u000aconsistent with focal cyst rupture.\u000aComplex sclerosing adenosis with associated microcalcifications.\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia.\u000aNOTE: Atypical ductal hyperplasia is best evaluated on surgical excision.\u000aDr. Lester has seen this case and concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 53 year old female with left breast 10 mm calcification cluster.\u000aOperation: 11g stereotactic breast core biopsy\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: Core samples (2 yellow cassettes - "A" with calcifications\u000aand "B")\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by RICHARDSON,ANDREA,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin, labeled with the patient's name, unit\u000anumber, and "left breast", and consists of two (2) yellow cassettes. One\u000acassette is labeled "A" with calcifications and contains multiple, soft,\u000afragmented, yellow to white tissue cores (2.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate).\u000aThe other cassette is labeled "B" and contains similar tissue fragments (2.6 x\u000a1.0 x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate).\u000aMicro 1: "A", multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 2: "B", multi frags, ESS.\u000akks\u000aReports to: MEYER, JACK, E. M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY(IES)\u000a[report_end]
