VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Skin, plastic repair\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: LIFEI GUO M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Li Chen, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jacqueline M William, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. RIGHT BREAST, SCAR:\u000aSkin with scarring and foreign body giant cell reaction\u000ato suture material, negative for tumor.\u000aB. SKIN, RIGHT UPPER ARM, BIOPSY:\u000aSkin with a lichenoid tissue reaction; see NOTE.\u000aImmunohistochemical studies performed at BWH show that the\u000alichenoid inflammatory infiltrate is composed primarily of small REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID+ T cells\u000a(REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID>REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)that express REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and are predominantly\u000anegative for REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID. Scattered admixed REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID+/REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDa+ B cells\u000aare seen and are negative for REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID expression.\u000aA PAS-D stain is negative for fungal organisms.\u000aNOTE: The morphologic and immunophenotypic findings favor a reactive process\u000asuch as a hypersensitivity reaction. Clinical correlation is needed.\u000a(Clinical history: Follicular lymphoma and DCIS, status post right breast\u000asimple mastectomy)\u000aNOTE: This case was also reviewed by Dr. E. Velasquez of the Dermatopathology\u000aService, who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Not given.\u000aOperation: Not given.\u000aOperative Findings: Not given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Acquired right breast implant.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right breast scar\u000aB/2. Right upper arm lesion\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Right breast scar", consists of a tan unremarkable\u000airregular fragment of tan-white skin (5.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which is submitted\u000ain toto.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 frag, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. Right upper arm lesion", consists of an unoriented tan\u000askin ellipse (1.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE (depth) cm), with a pink-tan irregular\u000aill-defined macule (0.3 x REMOVED_DATE cm), REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest radial margin. The\u000adeep margin is inked blue and the specimen is serially sectioned and submitted\u000ain its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Tips, en face, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Central cross sections, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000acp/elw\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jacqueline M William M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed\u000aon Tuesday REMOVED_MONTH 30, 2009 at 00:00:39AM\u000a[report_end]
