VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JAMES D IGLEHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Vinodh Pillai, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Andrea L Richardson, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA/1. LEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISIONAL BIOPSY FOR MICROCALCIFICATIONS:\u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA is focally present (see NOTE).\u000aMicrocalcifications are associated with columnar cell change and normal breast\u000atissue.\u000aNo evidence of malignancy is present.\u000aNOTE: The atypical ductal hyperplasia involves a single, large duct profile\u000awith prominent pagetoid spread. Immunohistochemical staining performed at BWH\u000ashows the ADH is positive for E-cadherin, supporting that the proliferation is\u000aductal in origin.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Micro Ca++ in left breast.\u000aOperation: Biopsy of the left breast - needle localization.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: None given.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) left breast needle localization, long/lateral, short/superior (fs).\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aMicrocalcifications and intact wire in specimen. SHS\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in one part, labeled with the patient's name,\u000aunit number, and "left breast biopsy after needle local, long/lateral,\u000ashort/superior", and consists of a REMOVED_DATE (superior to inferior x REMOVED_DATE (medial to\u000alateral) x REMOVED_DATE anterior to posterior cm breast biopsy with a localization wire\u000ain place and a short suture marking the superior margin and a long suture\u000amarking the lateral margin. An accompanying specimen radiograph demonstrates a\u000atight cluster of calcifications in the central portion of the specimen,\u000aadjacent to the localization wire. The specimen is inked per pathology\u000aprotocol. The specimen is inked by the surgeon per pathology protocol for the\u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/ orange. The tissue consists of dense\u000afibrous parenchyma (75%) and adipose tissue (25%). The specimen is entirely\u000asubmitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin, perpendicular sections, 6 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Body to include area of calcifications in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, multi frags\u000atotal, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, perpendicular sections, 4 frags, ESS.\u000almc/lp\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aThursday REMOVED_MONTH 10, 2009 at 04:48:54PM\u000a[report_end]
