VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: DIANE C LOCKHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Oname O. Burlingame, M.D., Ph.D.; Martin C Chang, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Deborah A Dillon, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS AND A CLIP:\u000aColumnar cell hyperplasia with ATYPIA.\u000aRadial sclerosing lesions, florid epithelial hyperplasia, and columnar cell\u000achanges with associated microcalcifications.\u000aPseudoangiomatous stromal hyperplasia.\u000aNOTE: Specimen mammography was performed, noting no clip present in the\u000aspecimen. The previous core needle biopsy (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) revealed atypical ductal\u000ahyperplasia. No clip or biopsy site changes are seen upon gross and microscopic\u000aexamination of the excision specimen. Specimen was entirely submitted for\u000ahistologic examination. Radiographic correlation is suggested.\u000aB. LEFT BREAST, EXCISION OF ADDITIONAL TISSUE:\u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, with focal pagetoid spread, is present (blocks 6,\u000a7, 11, 13; see note).\u000aColumnar cell hyperplasia with ATYPIA.\u000aSclerosing adenosis, radial sclerosing lesions, florid epithelial hyperplasia,\u000acolumnar cell changes and adenosis with associated microcalcifications.\u000aApocrine cysts.\u000aNOTE: Immunohistochemistry for E-cadherin demonstrates retention of membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in ADH.\u000aSpecimen mammography was performed, noting no clip present in the specimen. No\u000aclip or biopsy site changes are seen upon gross and microscopic examination of\u000athe excision specimen. Specimen was entirely submitted for histologic\u000aexamination. Radiographic correlation is suggested.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 49-year-old woman status post left stereotactic core biopsy for\u000amicrocalcifications on screening, which returned positive for ADH.\u000aOperation: Left needle localization breast biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left abnormal mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast tissue, suture: to secure wire.\u000aB/2. Additional left breast biopsy tissue - short/superior, long/lateral.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aSpecimen #1: Wire and ? few Ca++. OR called by Dr. Noroozian/SR.\u000aSpecimen #2: OR called. No clip or wire in 2nd specimen; few punctate calcs.\u000a/SR\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, unit number.\u000aPart A, "#1. Left breast tissue", consists of a breast lumpectomy specimen (3.6\u000acm superior-inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm medial-lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm anterior-posterior) with a\u000asurgical suture securing a needle localization wire to the mid anterior margin.\u000aThe specimen is inked presumptively per pathology protocol for the evaluation\u000aof margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green;\u000alateral/yellow; medial/orange.\u000aThe accompanying specimen radiograph demonstrates a wire and scant/faint\u000acalcifications within the specimen. The specimen consists of 50% fibrous and\u000a50% adipose tissue. No masses are identified. The specimen is serially\u000asectioned into 10 slices and entirely submitted from lateral to medial.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 1, lateral margin, perpendicular section to include area of\u000amicrocalcifications, REMOVED_DATE frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 2, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 3, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 4, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 5, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 6, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 7, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 8, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 9, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, perpendicular sections, 4 frags each, ESS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Additional left breast biopsy tissue", consists of a\u000ayellow/white/tan, cauterized, cup/C-shaped fragment of fibroadipose tissue (2.9\u000acm superior-inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm medial-lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm anterior-posterior) with a\u000ashort surgical stitch indicating superior and a long surgical stitch indicating\u000alateral. The tissue thickness ranges from REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE cm. An accompanying\u000aspecimen radiograph shows few punctate classifications with no clip or wire\u000apresent. Per the surgeon's operative note, the concave surface is contiguous\u000awith the above excision (Part A). It therefore represents a posterior\u000ahemispheric shell, including final superior, medial, lateral, and inferior\u000amargins.\u000aThe specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. Then anterior margin corresponds to the concave surface. The\u000aspecimen is separated into superior and inferior halves. Both specimen halves\u000aare serially sectioned from lateral to medial, to demonstrate fibroadipose\u000atissue with no grossly apparent mass lesions. The entire specimen is submitted\u000afor histologic examination.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior half of cup, sequentially submitted lateral to medial,\u000a1-2 frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior half of cup, sequentially submitted lateral to medial,\u000a2-3 frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin (red ink only), perpendicular, (originally adjacent\u000ato fragments in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aOOB/lp\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
