VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: DIANE C LOCKHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Agoston Tony Agoston, M.D., Ph.D.; Martin C Chang, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jason L Hornick, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT BREAST, 12 O'CLOCK WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION:\u000aIntraductal papilloma (0.6 cm) with atypia; see NOTE.\u000aHyalinized stromal nodule (0.5 cm) with calcifications.\u000aNOTE: Two microscopic foci of moderate to severe cytologic atypia are present\u000a(0.15 cm and REMOVED_DATE cm). The atypia is confined to the papilloma and focally\u000aextends to within REMOVED_DATE cm of the inferomedial margin. Calcifications, prior\u000aexcision site, and apocrine metaplasia are also associated with the papilloma.\u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. D. Dillon, Breast Pathology Service, who\u000aconcurs.\u000aB. LEFT BREAST, 6 O'CLOCK EXCISION:\u000aIntraductal papilloma (0.5 cm) with associated periductal chronic\u000ainflammation and duct ectasia.\u000aFocal fibroadenomatoid chance.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 77-year-old woman with past history of left breast biopsy with\u000adocumented papillomas found on screening exam to have new nodular density, left\u000abreast at 12 o'clock. Clinical exam showed single pore clear discharge from 6\u000ao'clock pore and abnormal ductogram.\u000aOperation: Left duct excision, left wire localized breast biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left abnormal mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast 12 o'clock wire localized - FS - Mammo. Tissue - short\u000asuperior / long lateral.\u000aB/2. Left breast 6 o'clock - short superior, long lateral, purple stitch on the\u000aduct.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aThe clip and intact wire are in the specimen.\u000aCalled to Dr. Lockhart in OR. Signature: EY 10:45 AM\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Left breast 12 o'clock wire loc. tissue, short sup, long\u000alat", consists of an irregular portion of yellow lobulated fibrofatty tissue\u000a(4.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), with a long suture designating lateral, and a short\u000asuture designating superior. The specimen is received previously inked per\u000apathology protocol as anterior blue, posterior black, superior red, inferior\u000agreen, lateral yellow, and medial orange. There is an inserted needle\u000alocalization wire, as displayed on the accompanying radiograph, which enters\u000aalong the anterior margin, and does not exit the specimen, however, traverses\u000ato the lateral/posterior margin. The specimen is serially sectioned to reveal\u000aa well-healed biopsy cavity (0.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which contains a surgical\u000aclip (as displayed on the accompanying radiograph). The cavity is REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior\u000amargin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin, and REMOVED_DATE \u000acm from the lateral margin. The cavity is surrounded by yellow lobulated\u000aadipose tissue, with no discrete masses or lesions identified. The remaining\u000acut surface displays 99% yellow adipose tissue, and 1% white fibrous tissue,\u000awith no additional discrete masses or lesions identified. The specimen is\u000asequentially submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, radially, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, with the cavity in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1 frag\u000aeach, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, with the cavity in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID,\u000acontaining the surgical clip, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, radially, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. Left breast 6 o'clock, short superior, long lateral,\u000apurple stitch on duct", consists of an irregular portion of yellow lobulated\u000afibrofatty tissue (3.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), with a long suture designating lateral,\u000aa short suture designating superior, and a purple suture designating the duct.\u000aThe specimen is received previously inked per pathology protocol as anterior\u000ablue, posterior black, superior red, inferior green, lateral yellow, and medial\u000aorange. The previously described purple colored suture designating the duct is\u000alocated on the anterior blue-inked margin. The specimen is serially sectioned\u000ato reveal 80% yellow lobulated adipose tissue, and 20% white fibrous tissue,\u000awith no discrete masses or lesions identified. The specimen is sequentially\u000asubmitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior margin, radially, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Posterior margin, radially, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000acp/elw\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jason L Hornick M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aThursday May 21, 2009 at 06:54:57PM\u000a[report_end]
