VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY(IES)\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aLESTER, SUSAN, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by LESTER,SUSAN,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, 11 G STEREOTACTIC CORE NEEDLE BIOPSIES FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA (best seen in block #1; original levels;\u000aadditional levels examined) (see note).\u000aApocrine cysts.\u000aCalcifications are associated with adenosis and ADH.\u000aNote: There is a single duct filled by a monotonous population of cells\u000awith low grade nuclei and a cribriform pattern, present in the original\u000alevels of block #1. This area is absent on the deeper levels.\u000aADH would be evaluated best by excision.\u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. Andrea Richardson who concurs with this\u000ainterpretation.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Right breast core biopsy, 8 mm calcification cluster.\u000aOperation: 11 G Stereotactic.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: 1) Right breast core samples (blue cassette) - calcifications\u000ain "A".\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by PREISINGER,ELIZABETH ANN,M.D.\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin, labeled with the patient's name, unit\u000anumber, and "Right breast", and consists of two (2) blue cassettes. One\u000acassette is labeled "A Ca++" and contains multiple, soft, fragmented, yellow to\u000awhite tissue cores (1.7 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm cm in aggregate). The other cassette is\u000alabeled "B" and contains similar tissue fragments (2.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in\u000aaggregate).\u000aMicro 1: "A Ca++", multiple frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 2: "B", multiple frags, ESS.\u000akks\u000aReports to: MEYER, JACK, E. M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY(IES)\u000a[report_end]
