VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Skin biopsy (other than cyst, tag, debrid or repair\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: HARLEY A HAYNES M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Marianna B Ruzinova, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Scott R Granter, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. SKIN, LEFT SCALP, PUNCH:\u000a- Dermal mucin deposition and pigment incontinence.\u000a- Mostly anagen and one catagen follicle are seen.\u000a- Multiple levels examined.\u000aNOTE: The findings are nonspecific and suggestive of post-inflammatory\u000achanges. A repeat biopsy on an active lesion may be more informative and is\u000aadvised, if clinically appropriate.\u000aB. SKIN, RIGHT BREAST, PUNCH:\u000a- Sparse perivascular chronic inflammation with dermal mucin deposition.\u000aNOTE: The changes are nonspecific but raise the possibility of connective\u000atissue disease, including tumid lupus erythematosus, reticular erythematous\u000amucinosis, and delayed-type hypersensitivity reactions. Serologic testing to\u000aexclude Lyme disease is advised, if clinically appropriate. Clinical\u000acorrelation is needed.\u000aC. SKIN, LEFT SHOULDER, PUNCH:\u000aThis specimen shows similar features to those in specimen "B". In addition,\u000ahowever, at the base of the biopsy is a nodular lymphocytic aggregate that is\u000aof uncertain biological significance. This finding raises the possibility\u000adeeper more significant pathology, possibly lupus profundus but these biopsies\u000aare too superficial for definitive evaluation. A deeper biopsy to allow for\u000amore definitive diagnosis is advised if clinically significant. A silver stain\u000ais negative for spirochetes.\u000aCase reviewed at Dermatopathology Division Staff Conference.\u000aCase discussed with Dr. Haynes.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 50-year-old F with a 31-year history of DLE on Plaquenil and\u000aquinacrine, who presents with alopecia and erythematous patches on L shoulder\u000aand R breast.\u000aOperation: A) 4-mm Punch; B) 3-mm Punch; C) 3-mm Punch.\u000aOperative Findings:\u000aA) Patch of alopecia, non-erythematous, non-scarring L scalp.\u000aB) R breast erythematous patch.\u000aC) L shoulder erythematous patch.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: A) Alopecia areata vs. DLE; B/C) DLE.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Skin: L scalp.\u000aB/2. Skin: R breast.\u000aC/3. Skin: L shoulder.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin, in three parts, each labeled with the\u000apatient's name and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Left scalp", consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm (depth)\u000atan/brown hair-bearing circular skin, inked and embedded in toto.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 frag, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. Right breast", consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm (depth)\u000alight tan circular skin, inked and embedded in toto.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 frag, ESS.\u000aPart C, labeled "#3. Left shoulder", consists of a REMOVED_DATE cm diameter x REMOVED_DATE cm\u000adepth light tan circular skin, inked and embedded in toto.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 frag, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Scott R Granter M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
