ge. The accompanying radiograph demonstrates a wire with a\u000asuperimposed clip and faint nearby calcifications. The specimen is sectioned\u000asuperiorly to inferiorly and reveals a firm white fibrotic area (2.4 x REMOVED_DATE x\u000a1.2 cm) abutting the superior, medial, and posterior margins, and is REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom the anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral margin and REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000ainferior margin. There is also a clip and suspected calcification that is\u000afound approximately REMOVED_DATE cm from this firm fibrotic area. The remainder of the\u000aspecimen (approximately 70%) is composed of predominantly adipose tissue.\u000aSpecimen entirely submitted for histologic examination.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin, REMOVED_DATE frags per block, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Serially sectioned specimen from superior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE frags\u000aper block, ESS. Note: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID are adjacent to the clip, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID contains\u000asuspected calcifications.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, 2 frags per block, ESS.\u000aTA/lp\u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-07-REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
