VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aLESTER, SUSAN, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by LESTER,SUSAN,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aLEFT BREAST, SUBAREOLAR, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A MASS:\u000aSUBAREOLAR SCLEROSING PAPILLARY DUCT HYPERPLASIA WITH ATYPIA, REMOVED_DATE cm, with\u000aloss of myoepithelial cells around the periphery (see note).\u000aThe lesion is < REMOVED_DATE cm from the nearest margin (not oriented).\u000aStromal changes consistent with the prior needle biopsy.\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies are performed with the following results:\u000ap63/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID PRESERVATION OF MYOEPITHELIAL CELLS IN\u000aTHE CENTER OF THE LESION WITH LOSS\u000aAROUND THE PERIPHERY\u000aSmooth muscle alpha actin SAME AS REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aCalponin SAME AS REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE IN CENTER, weaker in\u000aperiphery\u000aProgesterone receptor SAME AS ER\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu NEGATIVE (0)\u000a34betaREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID POSITIVE, DIFFUSE\u000aNeuron specific enolase POSITIVE - WEAK\u000aChromogranin NEGATIVE\u000aSynaptophysin NEGATIVE\u000aNote: An unusual feature of this lesion is the loss of myoepithelial\u000acells in small nests of epithelial cells around the periphery of the\u000alesion. Some of these nests of cells appear to infiltrate around small\u000aducts. In some areas, these nests are associated with stromal changes and\u000asuggest epithelial displacement due to the prior needle biopsy. Other\u000aareas are not associated with such stromal changes and raised the\u000apossibility of invasion. However, a pattern of multifocal invasion\u000aassociated with a benign lesion would be highly unusual.\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies show strong immunoreactivity for cytokeratin\u000a34betaREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and weak or absent immunoreactivity for neuroendocrine markers.\u000aThis pattern is typical for hyperplasia and would be not be characteristic\u000afor an unusual spindle cell carcinoma in situ.\u000aThe case was sent to Dr. P.P. Rosen who performed additional\u000aimmunoperoxidase studies (see his complete report below). He states that\u000abasement membrane is retained throughout the lesion. The cytologic\u000aappearance of the cells at the periphery is similar to that in the center\u000aof the lesion and he concludes that the lesion is not invasive carcinoma\u000aand he interprets the lesion as "subareolar sclerosing papillary duct\u000ahyperplasia with atypia".\u000aCONSULTATION REPORT FROM DR. P.P. ROSEN (New York - Presbyterian Hospital, New\u000aYork, NY; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; REMOVED_DATE ):\u000aThese slides show a complex sclerosing papillary lesion that in this location\u000ais best classified as subareolar sclerosing papillary duct hyperplasia with\u000aatypia. The issue of whether carcinoma has arisen in this tumor is difficult\u000ato resolve. Here, as is all too often the case today, we are asked to make a\u000adiagnosis in a lesion that has been substantially distorted by an antecedent\u000acore biopsy. Reactive changes and epithelial displacement due to the earlier\u000abiopsy are clearly present. I agree that there are some foci where expansile\u000agrowth at the perimeter appears to be intrinsic to the lesion and not\u000anecessarily attributable to the earlier procedure. Loss of myoepithelium may\u000aoccur focally in sclerosing benign papillary lesions but in my experience this\u000atends to occur more prominantly centrally rather than at the periphery as seen\u000ain your case.\u000aIt is possible for carcinoma to arise in a subareolar sclerosing duct\u000ahyperplasia as described in Breast Pathology, 2nd edition, p. 95 [authored by\u000aDr. Rosen] and I am concerned it may be present in this case. To investigate\u000athis further I hope you will indulge me by providing six (6) superficial\u000aunstained recuts each from blocks "1", "2", and "3" on slides suitable for\u000aimmunostains.\u000a[Unstained slides were sent to Dr. Rosen.]\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID immunostaining on sections from blocks "1" to "3" show strong reactivity in\u000athe center of the lesion with attenuation and focal loss of reactivity at the\u000aperiphery. Basement membrane is retained throughout the entire lesion as\u000ademonstrated by laminin and/or collagen 4 reactivity. The H&E appearance of\u000athe hyperplastic epithelium in peripheral regions where myoepithelium is\u000adepleted does not differ appreciably from central regions where it is present.\u000aThe results are perplexing. Not having done these studies previously in a case\u000aof subareolar sclerosing papillary duct hyperplasia I am inclined to be\u000aguided by the H&E appearance of the lesion, which I interpret as atypical\u000ahyperplasia.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: REMOVED_DATE cm retroareolar mass found on screening mammography. A core\u000aneedle biopsy revealed only normal tissue.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast mass.\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT: Mass in specimen. Dr. L. Twohig\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: 1. Left needle localization breast biopsy.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by KWAAN,MARY R.,M.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name and unit\u000anumber, with no marking sutures and is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm portion of breast\u000atissue with a wire protruding into the specimen. An accompanying specimen\u000aradiograph reveals a central density with slightly irregular margins and the\u000alocalization wire.\u000aThe specimen is inked black. There is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm white firm mass\u000awith slightly irregular borders. The mass is REMOVED_DATE cm from the nearest resection\u000amargin.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  sections through long axis of the mass, 1 frag each, RSS.\u000aMicro 3: sections showing closest margin and remainder of mass, 3 frags,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  remainder of specimen, 5 frags, ESS.\u000aNOTE: The entire specimen is submitted for microscopic examination.\u000aReports to: REILLY, RAYMOND, JOHN, M.B.B.C.H.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
