VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BRENT T SHOJI M.D.\u000aCASE: BG-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Briana Gleason, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Marisa R Nucci, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "LEFT BREAST, RE-EXCISION":\u000aHealing biopsy site.\u000aSkin with cicatrix.\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia.\u000aNo residual phyllodes tumor identified.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "UTERUS WITH LEFT TUBE AND OVARY" (861g):\u000aEndometrium:\u000aProliferative endometrium.\u000aMyometrium:\u000aAdenomyosis.\u000aLeiomyomata (intramural and subserosal, up to REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aSerosa:\u000aNo significant pathologic change.\u000aLeft fallopian tube:\u000aPeritubal adhesions.\u000aLeft ovary:\u000aCystic follicles.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "CERVIX (PORTION OF)":\u000aSquamous metaplasia.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Re-excision left breast phyllodes tumor excision site.\u000aOperation: Re-excision left breast biopsy site, TAH/LSO\u000aOperative Findings:\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Phyllodes tumor left breast, menorrhagia/fibroids\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a1. Re-excision left breast biopsy site\u000a2. Uterus with left tube and ovary\u000a3. Cervix (portion of)\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in 3 parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "re-excision left breast biopsy site", consists of a breast\u000aexcision specimen (7.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a skin ellipse (5.6 x REMOVED_DATE cm). The\u000aspecimen has been previously inked by the surgeon in five colors (red =\u000asuperior, green = inferior, black = posterior, orange = medial, and yellow =\u000alateral); the skin represents the anterior margin. There is a biopsy cavity\u000a(2.1 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with surrounding areas of fat necrosis and focal\u000ahemorrhage. The biopsy cavity is located REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest superior\u000amargin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest skin\u000amargin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest medial margin and REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest\u000alateral margin and REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest inferior margin. The breast\u000aparenchyma is composed of approximately 80% fibrous tissue and 20% adipose\u000atissue. Representative sections of biopsy cavity and each margin are\u000asubmitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - biopsy cavity superior margin, 5 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - biopsy cavity posterior margin, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - lateral margin, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - medial margin, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - inferior margin, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - skin, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aPart B, labeled "uterus, with left tube and ovary", is a 861-gram hysterectomy\u000aspecimen (17 x 12 x 11 cm) with attached left ovary (2.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) and\u000aleft fallopian tube (4.2 cm in length by REMOVED_DATE cm in diameter), and portion of\u000athe right fallopian tube (4.7 cm in length by REMOVED_DATE cm in diameter). The\u000acervical margin (3.9 x REMOVED_DATE cm) has a red, granular appearance. The\u000aendocervical canal (2.2 cm in length) has a tan herringbone appearance. The\u000aendometrial cavity (2.9 cm from cornu to cornu and REMOVED_DATE cm in length) is grossly\u000adistorted by approximately ten subserosal and intramural fibroids (up to REMOVED_DATE cm\u000ain diameter). The cut surface of the leiomyomata is white and whorled, without\u000aareas of hemorrhage or necrosis. The myometrium is thickened (maximum thickness\u000aof REMOVED_DATE cm). The left fallopian tube is patent and has a fimbriated end. The\u000aovary contains multiple corpora albicantia.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - anterior cervical margin, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - posterior cervical margin, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - LUS, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - anterior endometrium, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - posterior endometrium, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - serosa, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - representative sections of large leio, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - right fallopian tube segment, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - left fallopian tube fimbria cross sections, 5 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - ovary, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aPart C, labeled "cervix", consists of two fragments of ectocervix with\u000aglistening, white mucosa (3.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate).\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - representative sections of each fragment, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Briana Gleason M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Marisa R Nucci M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
