VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: KATHARINE CARTER M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Scott Lovitch, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Michelle S Hirsch, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST TISSUE, UPPER OUTER QUADRANT, EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS AND A\u000aCLIP:\u000aBreast tissue with ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, multifocal, some foci\u000awith apocrine features and associated calcifications (see COMMENT).\u000aColumnar cell change, adenosis, epithelial hyperplasia, intraductal\u000apapillomas, and calcifications associated with benign (non-atypical)\u000aepithelium are also present.\u000aCore biopsy site (see COMMENT).\u000aThere is no evidence of malignancy, including the evaluation of margins.\u000aCOMMENT: Multiple foci of atypical ductal hyperplasia (ADH) are\u000apresent, some associated with calcifications. However, there is no\u000aresidual ADH identified in the area of the prior biopsy site.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: S/p core biopsy at BWH REMOVED_DATE  showing ADH clip left: BWH radiology\u000arecommended excision of 2 other areas of calcifications left upper outer\u000aquadrant.\u000aOperation: Left mammographically directed left breast biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal mammogram left breast.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast tissue left upper outer quadrant.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Clip and 4 groups calcifications in specimen. E. P. Frost, MD\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in one part, labeled with the patient's name,\u000amedical record number, and "#1. Left breast tissue, left upper outer quadrant".\u000aIt consists of a single wire localized breast excision specimen (189 grams; REMOVED_DATE \u000acm from superior to inferior; REMOVED_DATE cm from lateral to medial; REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000aanterior to posterior) with two orienting sutures (short equals superior; long\u000aequals lateral). The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the\u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. There are three localization\u000awires that accompany the specimen: wire #1 enters the specimen superiorly\u000aapproximately REMOVED_DATE cm from the right lateral margin; wire #2 enters the specimen\u000asuperiorly and posteriorly; wire #3 enters the specimen inferiorly and exits\u000athe specimen posteriorly. The specimen is also accompanied by a radiograph,\u000awhich shows the three localization wires and associated calcifications near the\u000atips of the wires. Associated with the tip of wire #1 there is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x\u000a1.0 cm possible core biopsy site/biopsy cavity, which is located REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe superior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial, REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000alateral, REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior and REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin. There\u000ais a separate area of calcification in the superior aspect of the specimen near\u000athe prior biopsy clip; this is not associated with a mass or localization wire.\u000aThe clip is associated with an area of tan firm fibrous tissue (1.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm), which is located REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior, REMOVED_DATE \u000acm from the lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial, REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior, and REMOVED_DATE \u000acm from the posterior margin. In correlation with the radiograph, the\u000acalcifications appear to be closest to the superior margin. The fibrous tissue\u000ain the superior aspect of the specimen is entirely submitted for microscopic\u000aanalysis. The specimen is serially sectioned from lateral to medial.'\u000aThe gross specimen was reviewed with Dr. Susan Lester (Breast Pathology\u000aService).\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrous tissue and lateral margin, multi frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrous tissue associated with tip of wire #2 at superior margin,\u000amulti frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Possible biopsy cavity, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remainder of possible biopsy cavity and superior/posterior\u000amargins, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Area of clip and fibrous tissue and anterior, posterior, and\u000asuperior margins, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remainder of fibrous tissue along superior aspect of the\u000aspecimen from lateral to medial (medial part), 1 frag each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, 4 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Random fibrous tissue inferolateral part of the specimen, 1 frag,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Random fibrous tissue inferomedial part of the specimen, 1 frag,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Additional lateral margin, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000asek/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Michelle S Hirsch M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a8/1/2007\u000a[report_end]
