VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Stephanie Schulte, M.D.; Rachel L Rucker-Schmidt, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Alessandra F Nascimento, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aSEVERELY ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, bordering on DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU,\u000acribriform type, focally involving a papilloma, see NOTE.\u000aThe atypical focus is present in one (1) of six (6) blocks, and involves an\u000aarea of at least REMOVED_DATE cm.\u000aThe atypical lesion is present at the lateral resection margin and has been\u000asurgically transected.\u000aNormal breast tissue with calcifications.\u000aDuctal epithelial hyperplasia.\u000aNOTE: Due to severe cautery artifact, it is difficult to precisely classify\u000athis lesion; however, ducts with atypical cells arranged in a cribriform\u000aarchitecture are present. Immunoperoxidase stain for p63/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID highlights\u000amyoepithelial cells, supporting the above diagnosis. Re-excision to ensure\u000acomplete removal and to allow further evaluation is suggested.\u000aThis case was reviewed by Dr. Deborah Dillon, Breast Pathology Service, who\u000aconcurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 45-year-old female with L breast calcifications - L breast mammo,\u000a4/13/07, revealing "clustered punctate and amorphous calcification measuring 5\u000amm in UIQ - demonstrate suspicious abnormality - not amenable to core biopsy".\u000aOperation: L breast WL exc biopsy.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast tissue (long=lateral, short=superior, whipstitch on chest\u000awall).\u000aRADIOLOGY COMMENT:\u000aCa## and wire in specimen. PB -> ER in OR\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "#1. Left breast tissue short - superior, long - lateral, whipstitch -\u000achest wall", and consists of a portion of fibroadipose tissue (2.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm) with three orienting sutures ("long equals lateral; short equals superior;\u000awhipstitch on chest wall") and a needle localization wire in place (entering at\u000athe superior/posterior aspect and exiting at the lateral aspect). The specimen\u000ais inked by the surgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/ orange. The specimen is serially sectioned from superior to inferior,\u000aexhibiting approximately 80% tan/white unremarkable fibrostroma and 20% yellow\u000aadipose tissue. There is an accompanying radiograph which shows a wire and\u000acalcifications within the specimen. The specimen is entirely submitted for\u000ahistologic examination.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Breast (sequentially from superior to inferior), multi frags, ESS.\u000aNote: Calcifications in micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000adfi\u005cpab\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Alessandra F Nascimento M.D., Electronically signed on\u000a5/3/2007\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM:\u000aAt the request of Dr. Rhei, immunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin\u000afixed tissue with the following results for severely atypical ductal\u000ahyperplasia bordering on DCIS (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000ainvasive carcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor cells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only a\u000aportion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% of\u000atumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in >30% of\u000atumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum #1 by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
