h Dr. Dillon. The specimen is sliced into six slices, from superior to\u000ainferior, with the wire (and calcifications) located within slices 5 and 6.\u000aThe breast parenchyma is comprised of approximately 60% white fibrous tissue\u000athat is located within the inferior portion of the specimen. The remaining\u000abreast parenchyma is yellow, adipose tissue. The specimen is entirely\u000asubmitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Posterior, anterior, medial and lateral margins of slice 2, 1\u000afrag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial, posterior and anterior margins of slice 3, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior, posterior and lateral margins of slice 3, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial, posterior and anterior margins of slice 4, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior, posterior and lateral margins of slice 4, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior, posterior and medial margins of slice 5, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior, posterior and lateral margins of slice 5, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicros REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, perpendicular sections (near the wire), 3 frags\u000aeach,\u000aESS.\u000asak/MMM\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Samuel G Katz M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Michelle S Hirsch M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a2/1/2005\u000a[report_end]
