VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BRENT T SHOJI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Tanya Rege, M.D., Ph.D.; Wai Chin Foo, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Hejin P Hahn, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aBenign breast tissue with atypical ductal hyperplasia,\u000aintraductal papilloma, and calcifications in association\u000awith benign epithelium.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000a(Levels examined.)\u000aImmunohistochemical staining for E-cadherin was evaluated.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Hx Ca++ R breast.\u000aOperation: R needle loc br bx.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: R abnormal mammo.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Right breast tissue, short - superior, long - lateral --> mammo.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Ca++ and wire in specimen. RB phone to Shoji in OR.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "#1. R breast tissue SSS, LSL", and consists of an irregular fragment of\u000ayellow/tan to tan/pink fibroadipose tissue (8.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which is\u000aoriented per surgeon with two sutures; short equals superior and long equals\u000alateral. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of\u000amargins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green;\u000alateral/yellow; medial/orange. There is one needle localization wire in placed\u000aentering the anterosuperior aspect of the specimen with no clear exit point.\u000aThe specimen was previously radiographed, confirming the presence of multiple\u000acalcifications around the tip of the needle localization wire, which is\u000aembedded within the tissue in the inferoposterior aspect of the specimen. The\u000aspecimen is serially sectioned from lateral to medial into a total of nine\u000aslices (slice #1 equals lateral, slice #9 equals medial) to exhibit\u000aapproximately 30% dense/nodular white/tan fibrous stroma and 70% yellow/tan\u000alobulated adipose tissue. The majority of the fibrous stroma is in the\u000ainferior/central aspect of the specimen (as consistent with accompanying\u000aradiograph). The wire tip is embedded within the fibrous tissue in slice #6.\u000aRepresentative sections to include the fibrous tissue in the inferior/central\u000aaspect of the specimen in entirety are submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #1, lateral margin perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #2, fibrous tissue with inferior, posterior, and lateral\u000amargins, 1 frag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #3 fibrous tissue with inferior and posterior margins,\u000a1 frag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #4 fibrous tissue with inferior and posterior margins,\u000a1 frag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #5, fibrous tissue with anterior, inferior, and posterior\u000amargins, 1 frag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #6 fibrous tissue with posterior and inferior margins\u000a(site of needle localization wire tip), multi frags total, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #7 fibrous tissue with anterior, posterior, and inferior\u000amargins, 1 frag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slices #4 and #5 superior margin, 1 frag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #9, medial margin perpendicularly sectioned, 3 frags, RSS.\u000aPlease note that approximately 60% of the specimen is submitted.\u000aLM/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-07-REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Hejin P Hahn M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a8/29/2007\u000a[report_end]
