VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ROGER L CHRISTIAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Jason F Schmidt, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Jason L Hornick, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, EXCISIONAL BIOPSY WITH WIRE-LOCALIZATION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU and atypical lobular hyperplasia with pagetoid\u000aspread around ducts.\u000aCalcifications associated with benign epithelium.\u000aNOTE: This case was also reviewed by Dr. A. Richardson, Breast Pathology\u000aService, who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Microcalcs left breast\u000aOperation: None given\u000aOperative Findings: None given\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Microcalcifications left breast\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a1. Tissue from left breast - sutures: long lateral, short superior, specimen\u000ainked; to mammo\u000aRADIOGRAPH EVALUATION:\u000aCalcifications and hookwire in specimen - no prior bx diagnosis\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient?s name, unit number,\u000aand "1. tissue from left breast - sutures: long lateral, short superior,\u000aspecimen inked", and consists of a wire-localized breast excision specimen\u000a(superior-inferior REMOVED_DATE cm, medial-lateral REMOVED_DATE cm, anterior-posterior REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aTwo orienting sutures are present and designated by the surgeon as long=lateral\u000aand short=superior. An accompanying radiograph demonstrates a localization wire\u000apassing through a radio-opaque mass with a focus of microcalcifications near\u000athe tip. Within the specimen there is a localization wire that passes from the\u000alateral to the medial margin. The specimen was inked by the surgeon per\u000apathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The\u000ablue and black inks are difficult to identify grossly. No discrete lesions are\u000aidentified. The specimen is composed of approximately 60% adipose tissue\u000aadmixed with approximately 40% fibrous tissue. The specimen is entirely\u000asubmitted from medial to lateral.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: left breast, 1 frag each, ESS\u000aJFS/jp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Jason F Schmidt M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Review by Jason L Hornick M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jason L Hornick M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a1/20/2005\u000a[report_end]
