VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: DIANE C LOCKHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: David C. Park, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Jeffrey F Krane, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. SPECIMEN DESIGNATED "LEFT BREAST MASS":\u000aDuct ectasia with mild epithelial hyperplasia, periductular fibrosis,\u000achronic inflammation, hemosiderin deposition, and foreign body giant\u000acell reaction.\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia with pagetoid spread (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aE-cadherin immunostain shows loss of expression in lesional cells and\u000asupports the above diagnosis.\u000aThere is no invasive carcinoma seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aB. SPECIMEN DESIGNATED "ANTERIOR ABDOMINAL WALL MASS":\u000aMature adipose tissue consistent with lipoma (6.5 cm).\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 60 yo woman with left subareolar palp mass and h/o spontaneous nipple\u000adischarge although none could be found inducible on multiple clinical exams.\u000aHas recurrent soft tissue mass anterior abdominal wall requests excision.\u000aOperation: Excision L breast mass, excision anterior abd. wall soft tissue\u000amass.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: L breast mass, anterior abd. wall mass.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA. L breast mass short superior, long lateral\u000aB. Anterior abd wall mass.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, "#1. Left breast mass, short superior, long lateral", consists of a REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm excision of fibroadipose tissue, oriented by sutures designating\u000asuperior (short suture) and lateral (long suture) margins. The specimen is\u000ainked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange\u000a(inked by prosector). Sectioning demonstrates at least 95% tan/white lobulated\u000afibrodense tissue. A discrete mass is not seen. The entire specimen is\u000asubmitted, sequentially from lateral to medial, with perpendicular lateral\u000amargin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and perpendicular medial margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Multi frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Abdominal wall mass", consists of an aggregate of fragmented at\u000aleast partially encapsulated soft, lobulated adipose tissue (6.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm). Please note: True surgical margins cannot be assessed due to the\u000adisrupted, fragmented nature of the specimen. Cut surfaces are uniformly\u000atan/yellow and unremarkable, lacking hemorrhage, necrosis or calcification\u000agrossly. Representative sections (one per centimeter in greatest dimension)\u000aare submitted for routine processing.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Multi frags, RSS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aDB/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jeffrey F Krane M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a11/15/2007\u000a[report_end]
