en. The calcifications appear to be located in the lateral portion of\u000athe specimen. The specimen is bisected along the wire, however no gross\u000alesions are identified. There is significant leakage of ink into the\u000aparenchyma of the specimen. The specimen is entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: lateral portion of specimen at tip of localization wire, 1 frag,\u000aESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: central portion of bisected specimen, 2 frags, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: medial portion of first half of specimen, 1 frag, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: lateral portion of second half of specimen, 1 frag, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: mid-portion of second half of specimen, 2 frags, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: medial portion of second half of specimen, 1 frag, ESS\u000amah/jl\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Marian H Harris M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Xiaohua Qian M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a2/28/2005\u000a[report_end]
