VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST MASTECTOMY - PARTIAL OR SIMPLE\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aBRODSKY, GILBERT, M.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by BRODSKY,GILBERT,M.D.\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED PARTIAL MASTECTOMY AFTER NEEDLE CORE BIOPSY:\u000aSeverely atypical ductal hyperplasia, micropapillary and cribriform\u000apatterns, bordering on low grade ductal carcinoma in situ (blocks 6, 8),\u000awith associated microcalcifications, present less than REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000aanterior resection margin (block 8), see note.\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia, moderate, multifocal.\u000aMicrocysts.\u000aMicrocalcifications also present in association with benign epithelium.\u000aCore biopsy site with repair reaction.\u000aNOTE: The cribriform ductal proliferation focally qualitatively fulfills\u000acriteria for ductal carcinoma in situ, but does not fulfill quantitative\u000acriteria. (Selected slides reviewed with Dr. S. Schnitt)\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 51 year old postmenopausal female not on hormone replacement therapy,\u000awho on REMOVED_DATE  had a mammogram which demonstrated a new cluster of left breast\u000aupper outer quadrant microcalcifications for which histologic evaluation had\u000abeen recommended.\u000a10/13/98 core biopsy revealed highly atypical ductal proliferative lesion with\u000amicropapillations, apocrine features, and calcifications. Note: excision was\u000aworthy of consideration.\u000aOperation: Left breast double wire localization partial mastectomy.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast highly atypical ductal lesion.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: Left breast double wire localized partial mastectomy (short\u000asuture = superior, long suture = lateral, whip stitch = chest wall).\u000aO.R. CONSULTATION:\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "LEFT BREAST DOUBLE WIRE LOCALIZED TISSUE" (GROSS EXAM ONLY):\u000aThere is no evidence of malignancy at the level of the gross examination.\u000aRadiology: Calcifications are in resection specimen.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by WENG,ANDREW PHILIP,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in one (1) part, labeled with the patient's\u000aname, unit number, and "left breast double wire localized tissue," and consists\u000aof a single oriented fragment of tan/yellow fibroadipose tissue (6.2 cm from\u000ainferior to superior, REMOVED_DATE cm from medial to lateral, and REMOVED_DATE cm from anterior\u000ato posterior), with two (2) needle localization wires in place and three (3)\u000aorienting sutures designated short superior, long lateral, and whip deep. The\u000aspecimen margins are inked as follows: anterior = black, posterior = blue,\u000alateral = green, medial = red, inferior = yellow, and superior = orange. On\u000asection, there are no areas grossly suspicious for invasive carcinoma. The\u000aspecimen is entirely submitted. The specimen is accompanied by a specimen\u000aradiograph which demonstrates two (2) needle localization wires ? flanking an\u000aarea of parenchymal density with microcalcifications present.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  left breast biopsy, multiple frags, ESS.\u000aReports to: KAELIN, CAROLYN, MARY M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST MASTECTOMY - PARTIAL OR SIMPLE\u000a[report_end]
