VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Sentinel Lymph Nodes(s)\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: MEHRA GOLSHAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Cheryl Adackapara, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Jacqueline M William, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT AXILLARY SENTINEL LYMPH NODE I(Including FSA)\u000aOne (1) sentinel lymph node with reactive hyperplasia, negative for tumor (3 HE\u000alevels examined.\u000aImmunohistochemical studies performed at BWH reveal that the REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID+ B cells\u000apredominate in the follicular areas and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID+ T cells predominate in the\u000ainterfollicular areas. A REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID stain highlights germinal centers that are\u000anegative for Bcl-2 staining. These findings are consistent with a reactive\u000aprocess.\u000aB. LEFT AXILLARY SENTINEL LYMPH NODES II(Including FSB)\u000aTwo (2) sentinel lymph nodes with reactive hyperplasia, negative for tumor (3\u000aHE levels examined)\u000aC. LEFT BREAST, SIMPLE MASTECTOMY\u000aMULTIFOCAL INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA AND DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aINVASIVE CARCINOMA:\u000aMultifocal - REMOVED_DATE cm (In upper outer quadrant) and\u000a0.5 cm (In upper inner quadrant)\u000aHistologic type - Ductal\u000aHistologic grade (SBR) - I (Well Differentiated) (3 to 5 points)\u000aTubules - REMOVED_DATE points\u000aNuclear grade - REMOVED_DATE points\u000aMitotic counts - 1 point\u000aEIC - Negative\u000aLVI - Absent\u000aDermal LVI - Absent\u000aSkin invasion - Absent\u000aSkeletal muscle invasion - Not applicable\u000aFinal Margins (invasive)\u000aNegative - Deep.\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU - Present.\u000aTypes - Solid, cribriform,\u000aNuclear grade - Intermediate to high.\u000aCalcifications in DCIS - Present\u000aCentral necrosis in DCIS - Rare cells.\u000aNumber of blocks/total - REMOVED_DATE blocks\u000aMargins (DCIS)\u000aNegative - Deep\u000aOther findings: Atypical ductal hyperplasia, Atypical lobular hyperplasia,\u000aCore biopsy sites (3), Columnar cell change with associated\u000amicrocalcifications, skin with scar.\u000aOne(1) axillary lymph node, negative for tumor\u000aAJCC Classification (6th edition): REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDa REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID(sn), with two foci of invasion\u000a(includes parts A and B).\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER, PR, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) - Performed previously\u000aNOTE: There are two foci of invasive carcinoma. The REMOVED_DATE cm focus is located in\u000athe upper outer quadrant and likely corresponds to the 12:00 lesion biopsied on\u000a1/21/09 (see REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). The REMOVED_DATE cm focus of invasive ductal carcinoma is\u000alocated in the upper inner quadrant and corresponds to the 10 o'clock lesion\u000abiopsied on REMOVED_DATE  (see REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). Both areas are associated with core biopsy\u000asite changes and ductal carcinoma in situ. There is a third area of biopsy site\u000achanges corresponding to an area of MRI enhancement in the lower outer quadrant\u000abiopsied on REMOVED_DATE  (see REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). Atypical ductal hyperplasia was seen in\u000athis area which is concordant with the findings on the biopsy.\u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. Susan Lester, Breast Pathology Service, who\u000aconcurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 48 yo female with h/o atypical ductal hyperplasia bordering (left\u000abreast) on DCIS. New left breast cancer.\u000aOperation: 1. left axilla sentinel node biopsy\u000a2. left breast skin-sparing simple mastectomy\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: left breast cancer.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left axilla sentinel node I FS\u000aB/2. Left axilla sentinel node II FS\u000aC/3. Left breast skin sparing simple mastectomy, short superior, long lateral\u000aO.R. CONSULTATION:\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "1. LEFT AXILLARY SENTINEL NODE I (FSA):\u000aOne lymph node negative for tumor.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "2. LEFT AXILLA SENTINEL NODE II" (FSB):\u000aTwo lymph nodes negative for tumor.\u000aOR Consultation by: ALESSANDRA F. NASCIMENTO, M.D.\u000aResident: SCOTT LOVITCH, M.D., PH.D.\u000aThe senior physician certifies that he/she personally conducted a gross and/or\u000amicroscopic examination of the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed\u000athe rapid diagnos(es) related thereto.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, "left axilla sentinel node I", consists of one fragment of red/yellow\u000asoft tissue (2.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) containing one lymph node candidate (0.7 x REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm), which is bisected and entirely submitted for frozen section analysis\u000aas FSA.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Frozen section remnant FSA, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nonfrozen remnant, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aPart B, "left axilla sentinel node II", consists of one fragment of red/yellow\u000asoft tissue (2.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) containing two lymph node candidates (1.2 x\u000a1.0 x REMOVED_DATE cm and REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The REMOVED_DATE cm lymph node is inked blue and\u000aboth lymph nodes are submitted for frozen section analysis in their entirety as\u000aFSB.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Frozen section remnant FSB, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nonfrozen remnant of left axilla sentinel node II, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aPart C, "left breast skin sparing simple mastectomy, short superior, long\u000alateral", and consists of a 425-gram left simple mastectomy specimen (17.5 x\u000a16.0 x REMOVED_DATE cm) with orienting sutures to indicate superior margin with a short\u000asuture and lateral margin with a long suture. The specimen contains a\u000atan/white skin ellipse (4.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm) including a tan/white unremarkable\u000aeverted nipple (1.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with no grossly visible scars. There is no\u000aattached axillary tail. In the upper outer quadrant of the specimen is a\u000atan/white ill-defined firm area with hemorrhage, and a clip, consistent with a\u000aprevious biopsy site (1.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which is located REMOVED_DATE cm to the deep\u000amargin and REMOVED_DATE cm to the skin. This firm area is entirely submitted for\u000ahistological examination. In the lower outer quadrant is a tan/white\u000aill-defined firm nodule (2.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which is located REMOVED_DATE cm to the\u000aupper outer quadrant lesion and is located REMOVED_DATE cm from the deep margin and REMOVED_DATE \u000acm to the skin. Approximately 100% of this nodule is submitted for\u000ahistological examination.\u000aIn the lower inner quadrant is an ill-defined tan/white nodular area (2.0 x REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm), which is located REMOVED_DATE cm to the lower outer quadrant lesion and REMOVED_DATE \u000acm to the upper outer quadrant lesion, and is REMOVED_DATE cm to the deep margin and REMOVED_DATE \u000acm to the skin. Approximately 80% of this nodular area is submitted for\u000ahistological examination.\u000aIn the upper inner quadrant is a tan/red hemorrhagic nodule (1.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm) consistent with the previous biopsy site. This lesion is REMOVED_DATE cm to the\u000aupper outer quadrant lesion, REMOVED_DATE cm to the lower outer quadrant lesion, REMOVED_DATE cm\u000ato the lower inner quadrant lesion, REMOVED_DATE cm to the deep margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm to\u000athe skin. This lesion is entirely submitted for histological examination. The\u000alateral most adipose tissue in the specimen is sectioned to reveal two lymph\u000anode candidates measuring REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension. The remainder of the\u000abreast parenchyma is predominantly fatty.\u000aThe specimen is radiographed and serially sectioned. No other clips are\u000aidentified and areas possibly consistent with biopsy site in the upper outer\u000aquadrant and lower outer quadrant are submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nipple, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper outer quadrant lesion, including lesion to deep margin, 1\u000afrag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Tissue between upper outer quadrant and lower outer quadrant lesions,\u000a1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower outer quadrant lesion, 2 frags each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Intervening tissue between lower outer quadrant and lower inner\u000aquadrant lesions, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower inner quadrant lesion including to deep margin, 1 frag\u000aeach, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower inner quadrant lesion to skin, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Intervening tissue between lower inner quadrant lesion and upper\u000ainner quadrant lesions, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper inner quadrant lesion, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lymph node candidate, 1 inked blue, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper outer quadrant ?biopsy site, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower outer quadrant ? biopsy site, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower outer quadrant nodule, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: ?biopsy site, lower outer quadrant, multi frags, ESS.;\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aCAD/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jacqueline M William M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed\u000aon Thursday REMOVED_MONTH 23, 2009 at 08:08:52AM\u000a[report_end]
