VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: DIANE C LOCKHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-06-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Stacey A. Simons, M.D.; Peter M Sadow, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, LATERAL, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, with apocrine features and associated with\u000acolumnar cell change and calcifications, is present in 4 of 16 blocks as foci\u000ameasuring REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE cm (blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; levels examined) (see\u000anote). The ADH is positive for ER and PR and weakly positive for REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (2+)\u000a(block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aThe closest margin is lateral (0.2 cm).\u000aNote: The ADH closely resembles the patient's ADH in a prior excision\u000a(REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). The ADH is present in the area of radiologic calcifications.\u000aThe entire specimen is examined microscopically, including margins.\u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. Andrea Richardson, who concurs with this\u000ainterpretation.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, RE-EXCISION:\u000aBiopsy site changes.\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia (levels examined).\u000aSkin with scar.\u000aNote: No residual ADH is present. The entire specimen is examined\u000amicroscopically, including margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 54-year-old woman with severe ADH on right breast excisional biopsy\u000a09/08/2006. The patient has been recommended to have full excision of this\u000asite. A second area of microcalcifications has been identified at lateral and\u000ais also recommended for excision.\u000aOperation: Right breast re-excision, right needle localization breast biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast microcalcs with ADH.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right breast needle localization tissue.\u000aB/2. Right breast re-excision breast tissue.\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT:\u000aHook wire + calcifications. Called to Anita Minor, RN by Brent Townsend, MD,\u000aradiology.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen was received fresh, in two parts, labeled with the patient's name\u000aand unit number.\u000aPart A, "#1. Right breast needle loc", consists of a single wire localized\u000abreast excision specimen, which has been inked by the surgeon per pathology\u000aprotocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue;\u000asuperior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The specimen is\u000aaccompanied by a radiograph which shows a localization wire with a few\u000acalcifications at the tip of the wire. The specimen measures REMOVED_DATE cm S-I, REMOVED_DATE \u000acm M-L, and REMOVED_DATE cm A-P. The wire enters in the inferior aspect of the specimen\u000aand has no exit point. The specimen is sectioned from anterior to posterior\u000a(the only nearly preserved true opposing relationship on the specimen), into\u000atwelve slices (slice #1 anterior, slice #12 posterior). The wire passes\u000athrough slices #5 and #6, and the tip is found in slice #7 at the posteromedial\u000aaspect. There are no discrete masses within the specimen, and approximately\u000a50% of the specimen is composed of a single, confluent fibrotic region. The\u000aentire specimen is submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slices #1 and #2 (margins included A, M), 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #3 (S, M, I), 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #4 bisected (S, M, I, L), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #5 bisected (S, M, I, L), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #6 bisected, with fragment in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID further sectioned into 2\u000apieces (S, M, I, L), 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #7 bisected with tip found in cassette REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID (S, M, P, L, I),\u000a2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #8 bisected with fragments in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID further cut into 2\u000apieces (S, L, I, M, P), 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #9 bisected (S, L, I, M, P), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #10 (M, I, L, P), 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #11 and #12 (I, P), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Right breast: Re-excision breast tissue", consists of one breast\u000aexcision specimen, inked by the surgeon, with no wire or radiograph. There is\u000aa skin ellipse (1.9 x REMOVED_DATE cm), which is located at the junction of the anterior\u000aand inferior inked surfaces. The specimen is inked by the surgeon per\u000apathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The\u000askin ellipse has orange ink along much of the radial resection margin.\u000aDistortion of the specimen causes the medial aspect to oppose the superior\u000aaspect. The specimen is serially sectioned in a plane from this medial to\u000asuperior orientation, into eleven slices. The overall measurements are REMOVED_DATE x\u000a3.2 x REMOVED_DATE cm, however, the specimen measures REMOVED_DATE cm A-P, REMOVED_DATE cm M-L, and REMOVED_DATE cm\u000aS-I, with distortion. There is a hemorrhagic cavity (1.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm)\u000alocated in slices #2 through #6, located REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin, REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000aanterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral\u000amargin. There are no other discrete lesions, but greater than 90% of the\u000aremaining breast tissue is confluent fibrotic tissue. The specimen is entirely\u000asubmitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #1 (margins include M, I versus P), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #2 (M, I, P), 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #3 bisected (M, I, P), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #4 bisected (S, M, P, I, A), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #5 bisected (S, M, I, P, A), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #6 bisected (S, P, I, A, M), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #7 bisected with each half cut into A and B slices (S, A,\u000aL, I, P), 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #8 (S, P, L, M, I, ?A), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #9 (S, P, M, I, L), 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #10 (S, P, L, I versus A with additional fragments of I), 1\u000afrag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #11 (S, L), 1 frag, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-06-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aSAS/jp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a11/3/2006\u000a[report_end]
