VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aHIRSCH, MICHELLE, S., M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by HIRSCH,MICHELLE S.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aLEFT BREAST, TISSUE:\u000aIntraductal papilloma.\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia, confined to the papilloma.\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia, small focus.\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia.\u000aMicrocalcifications associated with papilloma.\u000aMargins are negative.\u000aThis case was reviewed by Dr. Susan Lester, Breast Pathology, who conurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: History of left nipple discharge with filling defect on ductogram.\u000aOperation: Left mammographically directed breast biopsy\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast discharge\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: #1. Left breast tissue (short = superior; long = lateral)\u000aRADIOLOGY: Density in specimen. D.N. Smith,MD\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by WU,DAVID,M.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "#1. left breast tissue, short stitch superior, long stitch lateral", and\u000aconsists of an oriented breast lumpectomy specimen (5.5 cm medial lateral, REMOVED_DATE \u000acm superior inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm anterior posterior). There is a metallic wire\u000alocated in the specimen within the inferior portion extending from medial to\u000alateral. The specimen is accompanied by a radiograph labeled with the\u000apatient's name and medical record number and a report that reads, "density in\u000aspecimen". The specimen is inked by the pathologist per pathology protocol\u000afor the evaluation of margins: posterior = black; anterior = blue; superior =\u000ared; inferior = green; lateral = yellow; medial = orange. The specimen is\u000aserially sectioned from medial to lateral to reveal a poorly circumscribed,\u000apalpable, firm area of white fibrous tissue with irregular borders in the\u000amedial portion of the specimen (2.6 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) that comes to within REMOVED_DATE \u000acm of the inferior, superior, posterior, and medial, REMOVED_DATE cm of the anterior,\u000aand greater than REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral margin. Medial to this fibrous\u000atissue and on the superior portion, there is a hemorrhagic, irregularly-shaped\u000afoci (1.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) that is less than REMOVED_DATE cm of the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE \u000acm to the anterior, REMOVED_DATE cm to the inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm to the posterior, and\u000agreater than 1 cm to the medial and lateral margins. The specimen is composed\u000aof approximately 40 to 50% fibrous tissue, the remainder adipose tissue.\u000aAdjacent to the hemorrhagic area is identified a small cyst (0.2 cm diameter).\u000aThe remainder of the tissue is yellow lobulated adipose tissue with no other\u000ahemorrhagic or necrosis. Ninety per cent (90%) of the total specimen, and 100%\u000aof the fibrous areas are submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  medial perpendicular margin, mult frags, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  fibrous and hemorrhagic tissues, mult frags, ESS\u000aMicro 18: lateral perpendicular margin, mult frags, ESS\u000adwu\u000aReports to: CARTER, KATHARINE M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
