VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ROGER L CHRISTIAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Matthew D. Stachler, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jane E. Brock, M.B., B.S., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A REMOVED_DATE CM MASS AT 10:00 WITH NIPPLE\u000aDISCHARGE:\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia, focal, see NOTE.\u000aFibroadenoma (0.6 cm).\u000aIntraductal papilloma with sclerosis (0.5 cm).\u000aCalcifications in normal epithelium and epithelial hyperplasia.\u000aThe margins were inked and evaluated for the final diagnosis.\u000aNOTE: E-cadherin stain highlights reduced membranous staining in ALH which\u000ainvolves the papilloma.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Nipple D/C, Lt MRI & US --> mass.\u000aOperation: Wire-directed excision.\u000aOperative Findings: See op note.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Nipple D/C, Lt MRI & US --> mass.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Tissue, left breast; long suture = lateral; short suture = superior.\u000aRADIOLOGY: The mass and intact wire are present in the specimen. De. Yeh spoke\u000awith Dr. Christian in OR.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "left breast tissue", consists of an irregular tan/yellow lobulated portion\u000aof fibroadipose breast tissue (3.5 cm medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm anterior to\u000aposterior, and REMOVED_DATE cm superior to inferior) with a short suture designating the\u000asuperior margin, and long suture designating the lateral margin. The specimen\u000aradiograph is reviewed in centricity and shows a wire and density in the\u000aspecimen. There is a needle localization wire in placed, which enters through\u000athe medial aspect of the specimen and exits through the lateral aspect of the\u000aspecimen. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol (superior red, inferior\u000agreen, medial orange, lateral yellow, anterior blue, posterior black), serially\u000asectioned from medial to lateral. The cut surface is tan/yellow and\u000ahomogeneous with focal areas of dense white fibrous tissue, which constitutes\u000aapproximately 70% of the breast parenchyma. No discrete lesions are grossly\u000aidentified. The specimen is submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, medial to lateral, 2 frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aBlocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID to include possible mass is noted per specimen radiograph. The\u000aspecimen radiograph is interpreted via Centricity and demonstrates the area of\u000ainterest marked by radiology.\u000aSEK/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aFriday REMOVED_MONTH 27, 2009 at 09:21:12PM\u000aADDENDUM:\u000aDeeper histological levels on the intraductal papilloma present (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) show the\u000aatypical E-cadherin negative cells noted within the papilloma develop an\u000aapocrine appearance and have higher nuclear grade than typically seen in\u000aclassic ALH. These cells are REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID keratin positive and ER and PR negative\u000a(ER and PR negativity is commonly seen in apocrine lesions) and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu\u000anegative (0). The atypical cells are confined to the papilloma and their\u000asignificance is unclear. Overall the diagnosis is best considered atypical\u000ahyperplasia with apocrine features confined to a papilloma. Dr. Susan Lester\u000aand Dr. Andrea Richardson both reviewed these additional slides and concur with\u000athe diagnosis.\u000aAddendum #1 by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aThursday REMOVED_MONTH 03, 2009 at 01:03:05PM\u000a[report_end]
