VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ROGER L CHRISTIAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Kremena V. Star, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Lucian R Chirieac, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. RIGHT BREAST TISSUE:\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia in the background of columnar\u000acell change with focal papillary change (best seen on REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aMicrocalcifications associated with atypical ductal hyperplasia\u000aand columnar cell change.\u000aApocrine cysts are present.\u000aB. RIGHT BREAST MIDDLE TISSUE:\u000aMicrocalcifications associated with columnar cell changes.\u000aBenign breast tissue with apocrine cysts.\u000aNOTE:\u000aThe case was reviewed by Dr. Amy Ly, Breast Pathology Service.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Microcalcs, Rt Ax accessory breast tissue.\u000aOperation: right breast wire-localized; excision biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: See op note.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Microcalcs, Rt Ax accessory breast tissue.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Rt breast tissue with short stitch - superior; long stitch - lateral.\u000aB/2) Rt breast middle tissue axillary site with stitch lateral.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "Rt breast tissue", consists of an irregular fragment of\u000afibroadipose tissue (7.5 cm M-L x REMOVED_DATE cm F-I x REMOVED_DATE cm A-P) with a short suture\u000adesignating superior and a long suture designating lateral per surgeon. The\u000aspecimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/ orange. The specimen is received with a needle localization wire\u000aentering the superior medial aspect of the specimen with no definitive exit.\u000aThe specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral to exhibit 20% dense\u000awhite fibrous tissue to 80% yellow lobulated adipose tissue. The fibrous\u000atissue is located along the access of the needle localization wire. No masses\u000aor lesions are identified. The specimen is submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular medial margin, 3 frag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Body, sequentially submitted from medial to lateral to include\u000afibrous area adjacent to needle localization wire (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), multi frags total,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular lateral margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "right breast middle tissue axillary site", consists of an\u000airregular fragment of fibroadipose tissue (3.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a stitch\u000adesignating lateral per surgeon. The lateral aspect is inked blue and the\u000aremaining surface is black. The specimen is serially sectioned to exhibit 70%\u000adense white fibrous tissues with 30% yellow lobulated adipose tissue. The\u000aspecimen is submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Multi frags total, ESS.\u000aJenC/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Lucian R Chirieac M.D., Electronically signed on Monday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 19, 2009 at 10:40:37AM\u000a[report_end]
