VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aKRANE, JEFFREY, FRED, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by KRANE,JEFFREY FRED,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, EXCISIONAL BIOPSY FOR CALCIFICATIONS AND A CLIP:\u000aMicrocalcifications associated with benign epithelium and with columnar\u000aalteration of lobules with prominent snouts and secretions.\u000aPrior biopsy site.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aCase reviewed with Dr. D. Dillon, Breast Pathology, who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 48 year old pre-menopausal woman with abnormal mammogram, revealing\u000a12 mm cluster of calcification in the upper/outer right breast. Core biopsy\u000a--> focal columnar alteration with flat epithelial atypia -- atypical lobular\u000ahyperplasia also present. Positive family history for breast cancer.\u000aOperation: Right breast wire localized biopsy.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal R mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: Right breast tissue with stitch marking short/superior,\u000along/lateral, whip stitch/deep margin (please check receptors).\u000aRadiograph report: Multiple Ca++s and one clip in specimen, C. Denison, M.D.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by WILLIAM,JACQUELINE M.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen was received fresh, labelled with the patien'ts name, unit number\u000aand "Right breast tissue" and consists of a fragment of fibroadipose tissue\u000a(3.1x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), with a long suture designating superior, a short suture\u000adesignating lateral and a whip stitch designating the deep margin.The specimen\u000awas inked by the surgeon as follows: red/superior, yellow/lateral,\u000agreen.inferior, black/medial, orange/anterior. Blue ink cannot be identified\u000aon the specimen. There is a wire localized within the specimen, and an\u000aaccompanying x-ray showing calcifications. The specimen was sectioned from\u000asuperior to inferior and is composed of 75% fibrous tissue, 25% adipose\u000atissue. No lesions are noted. The entire specimen was submitted.\u000aMicro #1-4: right breast tissue, with clip in #2, 8 frags, ESS.\u000ajaw/\u000aReports to: RHEI, ESTHER M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
