VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Li Chen, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jane E. Brock, M.B., B.S., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. RIGHT BREAST TISSUE, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU.\u000aAn E-cadherin stain performed highlights absent membranous staining in lobular\u000aneoplasia.\u000aNumerous microcalcifications associated with cysts and columnar cell change\u000awith ATPYIA\u000aApocrine cysts and sclerosing adenosis.\u000aThe margins were inked and evaluated for the final diagnosis.\u000aB. LEFT BREAST TISSUE, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aEXTENSIVE ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, (with and without associated\u000acalcifications), arising both in columnar cell change and involving numerous\u000aintraductal papillomas, see NOTE.\u000aATYPICAL LOBULAR HYPERPLASIA.\u000aNumerous microcalcifications associated with cysts and columnar cell change.\u000aThe margins were inked and evaluated for the final diagnosis.\u000aNOTE: The ADH is extensive and is focally severely atypical with areas of\u000acribriforming architecture. Dr. Susan Lester has reviewed selected slides and\u000aconcurs with the diagnosis.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 49-year-old premenopausal female whose REMOVED_DATE  mammograms demonstrate\u000aleft upper outer quadrant segmental pleomorphic calcifications and a grouped\u000aarea of punctate and amorphous calcifications in the axillary tail. Biopsy is\u000arecommended and neither are amenable to core biopsy.\u000aOperation: Bilateral wire localized biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Bilateral abnormal mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right breast tissue - short stitch superior, long stitch lateral,\u000awhipstitch chest wall margin.\u000aB/2. Left breast tissue - short stitch superior, long stitch lateral,\u000awhipstitch chest wall margin.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000a1. Specimen with calcification, hook and wire.\u000aCalled Dr. Rhei at 10:50 a.m.\u000aSignature: [illegible]\u000a2. Wires and calcifications in specimen (Raza).\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Rt breast tissue, short stitch superior, long lateral\u000awhipstitch chest wall margin", consists of an irregular portion of yellow\u000alobulated fibrofatty tissue (4.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), with a long suture\u000adesignating lateral, a short suture designating superior, and a whip suture\u000adesignating chest wall margin (posterior). The specimen is received previously\u000ainked per pathology protocol, as anterior blue, posterior black, superior red,\u000ainferior green, lateral yellow and medial orange. Please note that the short\u000asuture is placed on the margin, which is received inked blue (directly opposite\u000athe black inked margin with the whip suture designating chest wall margin,\u000aposterior). The surgeon is notified of the orientation discrepancy, and it is\u000anoted that now the short suture designates anterior superior. A needle\u000alocalization wire, as displayed on the accompanying radiograph, enters inferior\u000a- anterior margin, and exits at the superior - medial margin. The specimen is\u000aserially sectioned to reveal 98% dense white fibrous tissue, and 2% yellow\u000alobulated adipose tissue, with no discrete masses or lesions identified. The\u000aspecimen is sequentially submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, which may contain area of calcifications per the\u000aaccompanying radiograph, radially, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections from medial to lateral, with the\u000apossible area of calcifications per the accompanying radiograph in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1\u000afrag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, radially, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. Left breast tissue with short stitch superior and long\u000astitch lateral, whipstitch chest wall margin", consists of an irregular portion\u000aof yellow lobulated fibrofatty tissue (5.7 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), long suture\u000adesignating lateral, and short suture designating superior, and a whip suture\u000adesignating chest wall margin (posterior). The specimen is received previously\u000ainked per pathology protocol as the anterior blue, posterior black, superior\u000ared, inferior green, lateral yellow and medial orange. There are two inserted\u000aneedle localization wires (as displayed on the accompanying radiograph).\u000aNeedle localization wire #1 (arbitrarily designated by the prosector), enters\u000aand exits at the superior margin. Needle localization wire #2 (arbitrarily\u000adesignated by the prosector), enters the clear margins REMOVED_DATE cm inferior to\u000aneedle localization wire #1, and does not exit the specimen. The specimen is\u000aserially sectioned to reveal 98% dense white focally nodular fibrous tissue,\u000aand 2% yellow lobulated adipose tissue, with no discrete masses or lesions\u000aidentified. The specimen is sequentially submitted from superior to inferior\u000ain its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, fragmented, multi frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, bisected, with the possible area of\u000acalcifications on the accompanying radiograph, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, bisected, with the possible\u000acalcifications on the accompanying radiograph, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, bisected, with the possible area of\u000acalcifications in the accompanying radiograph, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, bisected, with the possible area of\u000acalcifications, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, bisected, with the possible area of\u000acalcifications, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, radially, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID 4 frags, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID 3 frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000acp/elw\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aSunday REMOVED_MONTH 28, 2009 at 07:04:19AM\u000a[report_end]
