VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Sentinel Lymph Nodes(s)\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ROGER L CHRISTIAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Kristin S. Ricci, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT SENTINEL LYMPH NODES (INCLUDING FROZEN SECTIONS):\u000aThree sentinel lymph nodes are free of carcinoma (3 H&E levels\u000aexamined.)\u000aREACTIVE CHANGES INCLUDING INTERFOLLICULAR HYPERPLASIA are present in\u000athe lymph nodes.\u000aThe lymph nodes show marked expansion of interfollicular spaces but\u000aotherwise preserved nodal architecture with multiple primary follicles\u000aand patent sinuses.\u000aImmunohistochemical studies performed on paraffin sections show\u000apreserved nodal architecture with REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDpositive B cells confined to\u000aprimary follicles and REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDpositive T cells that co-express REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID present predominantly in the expanded interfollicular zones. No\u000aaberrant reactivity of REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID or REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID is seen in B cell areas. Bcl-2 is\u000apositive in both T cells and primary follicle B cells. Cd10, Bcl-6,\u000aand Cyclin-D highlight only scattered cells. REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID highlights follicular\u000adendritic meshwork.\u000aNOTE: Overall, the findings are consistent with a REACTIVE PROCESS.\u000aDiagnostic features of involvement by a lymphoproliferative disorder are not\u000aseen. Clinical correlation is advised for further evaluation. This part of\u000athe case was reviewed by Dr. G. Pinkus, Hematopathology Service, who\u000aconcurs.\u000aRIGHT BREAST WIRE LOCALIZATION FOR A MASS AND A CLIP:\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA with ductal and lobular features, moderately\u000adifferentiated (modified Bloom-Richardson grade II/III), 1 cm, is present,\u000aassociated with a prior core needle biopsy site.\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is seen.\u000aNo carcinoma in situ is seen (EIC negative).\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia is present.\u000aThe margins are free of invasive carcinoma (< REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin-fixed tissue with\u000athe following results for invasive carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen Receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone Receptor POSITIVE\u000aHER-2/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0)\u000aAJCC Classification (6th edition) - REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDb REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID with two foci of invasion\u000a(see below).\u000aRIGHT BREAST, INFERIOR SHAVE MARGIN:\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA, modified Bloom-Richardson grade II/III,\u000aO.2 cm (Block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). An immunohistochemical study for p63 and\u000acytokeratin REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID confirms the absence of myoepithelial cells in the area\u000aof invasion.\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion identified.\u000aNo ductal carcinoma in situ is present (EIC negative).\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU is present. An immunoperoxidase study for\u000ae-cadherin stain confirms the absence of this marker.\u000aComplex sclerosing lesion (0.8 cm).\u000aThe separate focus of invasive carcinoma is REMOVED_DATE cm from the new\u000ayellow-inked and green-inked margins (inked by the surgeon).\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed with the following results for\u000ainvasive carcinoma:\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu NEGATIVE (0)\u000aE-cadherin POSITIVE\u000aNote: According to the operative report, there was a palpable mass in\u000athis specimen that might correlate with the location of a second MRI lesion\u000athat was approximately 1 cm from the known carcinoma in the main excision.\u000aThe complex sclerosing lesion may correlate with this lesion. The invasive\u000acarcinoma is very small and does not have a desmoplastic reaction. This\u000aappears to be a separate of focus of invasive carcinoma.\u000aClinical correlation is necessary to identify the orientation of the\u000ainked margins.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, LATERAL SHAVE MARGIN:\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Biopsy proven CA, RT breast.\u000aOperation: SNB, wire directed excision RT.\u000aOperative Findings: See op note.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Bx proven CA, RT breast.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA. Right sentinel node (FS)\u000aB. Right breast primary tumor site, long suture lateral, short superior, whip\u000astitch posterior (tissue painted incorrectly) red = inferior, green = superior.\u000aC. Inferior shave margin, right breast.\u000aD. Lateral shave margin, right breast\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aCalled to the OR: Mass, clip and intact wire i specimen radiograph. A Odulate\u000aREMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aO.R. CONSULTATION:\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "1. RIGHT SENTINEL NODE" (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lymph node (1) negative for metastatic tumor. Atypical\u000alymphocytic proliferation present. Tissue is submitted to\u000ahematopathology work-up.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lymph node (1) negative for metastatic carcinoma. Atypical\u000alymphocytic proliferation.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lymph node (1) negative for metastatic carcinoma. Atypical\u000alymphocytic proliferation.\u000aCase shown to HONG BO YU, M.D., PH.D. (Hematopathology).\u000aOR Consultation by: VANIA NOSE, M.D., PH.D.\u000aResident: GEORGE LIN, M.D., PH.D.; CHRISTIAN J. DAVIDSON, M.D.\u000aThe senior physician certifies that he/she personally conducted a gross and/or\u000amicroscopic examination of the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed\u000athe rapid diagnos(es) related thereto.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in four parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "right sentinel nodes FS", consists of firm rubbery fragments\u000aof soft tissue consistent with lymph nodes (ranging from REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm to\u000a2.6 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension). The specimens are bisected, and\u000asubmitted in their entirety for frozen section evaluation (largest lymph node\u000abisected, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; medium-sized lymph node bisected, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; and small\u000alymph node bisected, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID. The frozen section remnant REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID is submitted to Dr.\u000aPinkus lab for special studies. Remainder of the specimen is submitted in its\u000aentirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Frozen section remnant REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Frozen section remnant REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Frozen section remnant REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Primary tumor site R breast red equals inferior, green equals\u000asuperior, long suture lateral, short superior, whipstitch posterior", consists\u000aof an irregular portion of fibrofatty tissue (6.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), with a long\u000asuture designating lateral, a short suture designating superior, and a\u000awhipstitch designating posterior. The specimen is received previously inked,\u000aas follows: Superior green, inferior red, anterior blue, posterior black,\u000alateral yellow, and medial orange. (Please note that the specimen is inked\u000asuperior green and inferior red). There is an inserted needle localization\u000awire, which enters along the lateral margin, and does not exit the specimen.\u000aThe specimen is serially sectioned to reveal a white firm irregular ill-defined\u000amass (1.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000aanterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin (inked green), REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000ainferior margin (inked red), REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000alateral margin. A surgical clip is identified within the mass, as displayed on\u000athe accompanying radiograph. The remaining cut surface displays 98% yellow\u000alobulated adipose tissue, and 2% white fibrous tissue, with no additional\u000adiscrete masses or lesions identified. The specimen is sequentially submitted\u000ain its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, radially, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, with the mass in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1 frag\u000aeach, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, with the mass and the clip\u000aidentified in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, radially, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aPart C, "#3. Inferior shave margin, right breast", consists of a portion of\u000afibrofatty tissue (2.9 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The specimen is received partially\u000apreviously inked, displaying black/yellow and green colors with no designation\u000aas to the orientation. The opposite aspect is received not inked, and displays\u000aan undesignated black suture. This aspect is inked blue. The specimen is\u000aserially sectioned to reveal 90% yellow lobulated adipose tissue, and 10% white\u000afibrous tissue, with no discrete masses or lesions identified. The specimen is\u000asequentially submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 2 frags each, ESS.\u000aPart D, "#4. Lateral shave margin, right breast", consists of two portions of\u000afibrofatty tissue (averaging REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which are received previously\u000ainked yellow along one aspect. The opposite aspect of both specimens is\u000areceived not inked, and is inked blue by the prosector. The specimens are\u000aserially sectioned to reveal 90% yellow lobulated adipose tissue, and 10% white\u000afibrous tissue, with no discrete masses or lesions identified. The specimens\u000aare sequentially submitted in their entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 fibrofatty tissue, serially sectioned.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 fibrofatty tissue, serially sectioned, 2 frags each, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aCP/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a8/25/2008\u000a[report_end]
