VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast core needle biopsy\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ROGER L CHRISTIAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Qinghong Yang, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Deborah A Dillon, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, UPPER OUTER, 2:00, 14 G ULTRASOUND GUIDED CORE NEEDLE BIOPSIES FOR\u000aA MASS:\u000aINVASIVE LOBULAR CARCINOMA, well differentiated (modified Bloom-Richardson\u000agrade I/III), measuring at least REMOVED_DATE cm in size in this limited specimen.\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is seen in this limited specimen.\u000aNo definite in situ carcinoma is present in this limited specimen.\u000aNOTE: Immunohistochemistry for p63/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID confirms the absence of\u000amyoepithelium around foci of invasive carcinoma. Immunohistochemistry for\u000aE-cadherin confirms the absence of membrane immunoreactivity, supporting\u000alobular phenotype.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHISTORY: 64 y. o. female S/P R breast cancer. Diagnostic mammogram on REMOVED_DATE \u000ashowed a focal asymmetry in the left breast corresponding to an echogenic mass\u000a@ 2:00 on US.\u000aBREAST IMAGING FINDING:\u000a1. Left:\u000aLOCATION:\u000a1. Left: upper outer 2:00\u000aTYPE OF CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY:\u000a1. 14G Ultrasound\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a1. 14G Ultrasound # of cores - 6; time in formalin 4:45 p.m. - white cassette\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin, labeled with the patient's name, unit\u000anumber, and "left breast white cassette", consists of a white cassette\u000acontaining several cylindrical fibrofatty soft tissues (1.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in\u000aaggregate), which are submitted in toto.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Contents of white cassette, multi frags, ESS.\u000aJenC/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on Wednesday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 22, 2009 at 02:32:04PM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM (7/22/09):\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0 to 1+)\u000aNOTE: Normal epithelial cells show no immunoreactivity for progesterone\u000areceptor.\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not\u000ayet been determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but\u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a< 30% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>30% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aAddendum #1 by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on Wednesday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 22, 2009 at 02:36:32PM\u000a[report_end]
