lack;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. An\u000aembedded localization wire is present. An accompanying radiograph demonstrates\u000amicrocalcifications present in the specimen adjacent to the embedded wire.\u000aSectioning reveals a hemorrhagic area measuring approximately REMOVED_DATE cm in\u000agreatest dimension, compatible with a core biopsy site. The specimen is\u000aentirely submitted from superior to inferior.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: superior, slices 1 and 2, 2 frags, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice 3, 1 frag, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice 4, 1 frag, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice 5, 1 frag, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice 6, 1 frag, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice 7, 1 frag, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice 8, 1 frag, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: inferior, slices 9 and 10, 2 frags, ESS\u000aViv/jl\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aMicroscopic/Diagnostic Dictation by Vijay K Vanguri M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jason L Hornick M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a12/13/2004\u000a[report_end]
