VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JAMES D IGLEHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Elizabeth Amy Morgan, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Jason L Hornick, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED BIOPSY FOR A MASS AND A CLIP:\u000aArea of interlobular fibrosis (1.4 cm) associated with apocrine cysts,\u000apseudoangiomatous stromal hyperplasia and focal LOBULAR CARCINOMA IN\u000aSITU.\u000aCore biopsy site changes.\u000aThere is no invasive carcinoma seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Small density on mammo. Core showed PASH and LCIS.\u000aOperation: Left needle localized breast biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast ca.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast biopsy after needle loc, long lateral, short superior.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Mass, clip and wire in specimen. OR called LBT.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in one part, labeled with the patient's name,\u000aunit number, and "#1. Left breast biopsy after needle loc, long lateral, short\u000asuperior", and consists of a single lumpectomy specimen (anterior to posterior,\u000a6.2 cm; medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm; superior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm). The specimen\u000ais marked with sutures by the surgeon: Short equals superior, long equals\u000alateral. There is a wire entering in the lateral-posterior edge that marks a\u000aclip and a mass according to the accompanying radiograph. The specimen is\u000ainked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange.\u000aThe specimen is serially sectioned from medial to inferior-lateral to reveal a\u000afirm, irregularly-bordered, white/pink area measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. The\u000aclip is located at this area. The area grossly abuts the inferior margin and\u000athe anterior margin, is REMOVED_DATE cm to the lateral margin, is REMOVED_DATE cm to the\u000aposterior margin, and is greater than REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial and superior\u000amargins. There are also two areas of hemorrhage at the inferior border,\u000agreater than REMOVED_DATE cm from the white/pink area. The majority of the specimen\u000aconsists of adipose tissue with approximately 10% fibrous tissue. The entire\u000awhite/pink area, the hemorrhagic areas, and fibrous tissue are submitted for\u000ahistologic evaluation. Representative sections of margins are additionally\u000asubmitted. Approximately 50% of the specimen is submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, radially sectioned, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 5 slices containing white/pink area (bisected), clip in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1\u000afrag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrous area at posterior margin, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrous area at superior and posterior margins (adjacent to REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), 1\u000afrag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrous area at anterior margin, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrous area at superior margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Hemorrhagic area at inferior-posterior margin, 2 frags each,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrous areas at superior and inferior margins, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral-inferior margin, radially sectioned, multi frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-08-REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aem/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jason L Hornick M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a1/29/2008\u000a[report_end]
