VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aKRANE, JEFFREY, FRED, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by KRANE,JEFFREY FRED,M.D.,PH.D.\u000a1) LEFT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZATION FOR A CLIP:\u000aBreast tissue with epithelial hyperplasia.\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia (block 6).\u000aFibrocystic changes including cysts, dense interlobular fibrosis, and\u000aapocrine metaplasia.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000a2) SPECIMEN DESIGNATED "LEFT BREAST, ADDITIONAL SUPERIOR/ANTERIOR MARGIN":\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia with pagetoid spread (block 10).\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia (block 12).\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia.\u000aPrevious biopsy site and clip identified.\u000aFibrocystic changes including cysts, apocrine metaplasia, and\u000acalcifications in benign epithelium.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aNOTE: Case reviewed with Dr. A. Richardson, Breast Pathology, who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 51 year old female with atypical ductal hyperplasia on left breast\u000acore biopsy.\u000aOperation: Left breast wire localization biopsy\u000aClinical Diagnosis: ADH\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: #1) Left breast tissue with wire\u000a#2) More tissue from left breast, additional superior/anterior margin\u000aRADIOLOGY: Clip in specimen #2. S. Raza,MD\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by BUNDOCK,ELIZABETH A.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart one, labeled "#1. L. breast tissue with wire", consists of a portion of\u000afibroadipose tissue (3.5 x 3 x 1 cm) with a localizing wire in place. The\u000aspecimen is received alreading inked in pathology per pathology protocol for\u000athe evaluation of margins: posterior = black; anterior = blue; superior = red;\u000ainferior = green; lateral = yellow; medial = orange. Three short sutures (not\u000aused for orientation) are present holding together a transected part of the\u000aspecimen along the inferior margin. The specimen is sectioned at 3 mm\u000aintervals from lateral to medial revealing 90% of the specimen to be composed\u000aof rubbery white fibrous tissue, without firm foci suggestive of invasive\u000acarcinoma, and the remaining 10% being adipose tissue. The fibrous tissue\u000aextends to all but the superior and lateral margins. The specimen is entirely\u000asubmitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  breast tissue, 11 frags, ESS\u000aPart two, labeled "#2", consists of a portion of fibroadipose tissue (3.5 x REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm) which is received in pathology already inked per pathology protocol\u000afor the evaluation of margins: posterior = black; anterior = blue; superior =\u000ared; inferior = green; lateral = yellow; medial = orange. The specimen is also\u000areceived with a specimen radiograph which is reported as clip in specimen. The\u000aspecimen is serially sectioned at 3 mm intervals from lateral to medial\u000arevealing a specimen which is completely composed of soft rubbery white fibrous\u000atissue with no firm areas to suggest invasive carcinoma. The clip is\u000aidentified within the specimen near the inferior margin (submitted in cassette\u000a8). The specimen is entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  breast tissue, 11 frags, ESS\u000aeab\u000aReports to: LESNIKOSKI, BETH-ANN M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: SURGICAL MARGIN\u000a[report_end]
