VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BETH ANN LESNIKOSKI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Ann K Folkins, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Vania Nose, MD, PhD\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aSKIN EXCISION, DESIGNATED ?OLD KELOID SCAR:\u000aSkin with cicatrix.\u000aLEFT BREAST, DOUBLE WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR MULTIPLE AREAS OF\u000aCALCIFICATIONS:\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia (e.g. blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aSclerosing adenosis with numerous calcifications. Immunoperoxidase studies for\u000acytokeratin (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) and p63 document the presence of myoepithelial cells.\u000aApocrine cysts with calcifications.\u000aFibrosis and foreign body granulomas, consistent with prior biopsy site.\u000aNote: No carcinoma is seen after complete microscopic examination of the\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aThe calcifications do not appear to be specifically associated with the ADH or\u000aALH.\u000aThis case was reviewed by Dr. Susan Lester, who concurs with this\u000ainterpretation.\u000aLEFT UPPER EXTREMITY, SKIN EXCISION:\u000aDermatofibroma.\u000aNote: This specimen was reviewed by Dr. George Murphy, who concurs with this\u000ainterpretation.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: History of left breast LCIS and ADH, microcalcifications at site of\u000aprior excision and left upper area ulcerated skin lesion for months.\u000aOperation: Left breast wire localized biopsy REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, left skin excisional biopsy.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Microcalcifications left breast.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1 - old keloid scar.\u000aB/2 - left breast tissue with wires (frozen)\u000aC/3 - left upper extremity skin lesion (short/superior, long/lateral).\u000aRADIOGRAPHY REPORT: Several groups of Ca++ within specimen, 1 of which is\u000anear margin. Two intact hook wires. M. Lee called to OR REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, each labeled with the patient?s\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1 - old keloid scar left breast", consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x\u000a0.7 cm (depth) tan/white, vaguely nodular skin fragment. The subcutaneous\u000atissue is inked, and the entire specimen is submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: multiple frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2 - left breast tissue with wire REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm fibrofatty tissue fragment which is previously inked by the surgeon\u000aper the pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. An\u000aaccompanying specimen radiograph shows several groups of calcifications within\u000athe specimen, one of which is near the margin, along with two intact localizing\u000awires. One wire enters superiorly and exits posterior/medially, and the second\u000awire enters superior/anteriorly and exits inferomedially. The specimen\u000aconsists of dense white fibrous tissue in the area designated by the wires,\u000awithout definitive masses, lesions, or other abnormalities. Fibrous tissue\u000aextends to all margins in this area. The remainder of the specimen is\u000apredominantly fatty (75%), with a small amount of additional unremarkable\u000atan/white fibrous tissue. The entire specimen is submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: entire specimen, sequentially from medial to lateral (tissue\u000abetween localizing wire in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), multiple frags, ESS.\u000aPart C, labeled "#3 - left upper extremities skin lesion", consists of a REMOVED_DATE x\u000a1.3 x REMOVED_DATE (depth) cm tan/white, roughly ovoid skin fragment with a short suture\u000aalong one long axis designating superior, referred to as 12 o'clock, and a long\u000asuture at one tip designating the lateral, referred to as 3 o'clock. The 12\u000ao'clock half is inked green, the 6 o'clock half is inked blue. The skin\u000asurface demonstrates a linear well-healed scar along its length (at least REMOVED_DATE \u000acm). The specimen is inked and entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 3 o'clock tip, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 9 o'clock tip, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: remaining sequential cross-sections from 3 o'clock to 9\u000ao'clock, 2 frags each, ESS.\u000adb/lp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Vania Nose MD, PhD, Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
