VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aQIAN, XIAOHUA, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by QIAN,XIAOHUA,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZATION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia\u000aSclerosing adenosis\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination\u000aof the specimen, including margins.\u000aImmunostains for Calponin and p63 confirm the presence of myoepithelial\u000acells in a focus of sclerosing adenosis.\u000aNOTE: The case was also reviewed by Dr. S. Lester and D. Dillon (Breast\u000aPathology Service), who concur with the diagnosis.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Strong family history of breast cancer. Microcalcifications right\u000amammo.\u000aOperation: Needle-directed right breast biopsy.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast microcalcifications.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: 1. Right breast mass.\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT:\u000aCalcifications in specimen. Dr. Denison\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by KATZ,SAMUEL G, M.D., PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "Right breast tissue needle loc bx", and consists of a REMOVED_DATE cm (medial to\u000alateral) x REMOVED_DATE cm (inferior to superior) x REMOVED_DATE cm (anterior to posterior)\u000abreast biopsy with a localization wire in place. An accompanying specimen\u000aradiograph demonstrates a tight cluster of calfications near the localization\u000awire in the superior-medial portion of the specimen. The specimen was inked by\u000athe surgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. The specimen is serially sectioned from superior to inferior to\u000areveal approximately 60% dense, white fibrous parenchyma with the remaining\u000a40% a dark gray/yellow adipose tissue. The majority of fibrous tissue is\u000alocated closest to the superior and lateral borders. 100% of the fibrous tissue\u000ais submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  Superior tip margin, 4 frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  Sequential superior to inferior slices, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  Inferior margin, 9 frags, ESS.\u000asak\u000aReports to: LOCKHART, DIANE, CATHERINE M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
