VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Sentinel Lymph Nodes(s)\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ROGER L CHRISTIAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Julie D.R. Reimann, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Lucian Chirieac, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. SPECIMEN LABELED "RIGHT SENTINEL LYMPH NODES" (including REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aTwo (2) lymph nodes negative for tumor (3 HE levels examined).\u000aB. SPECIMEN LABELED "RIGHT BREAST", SIMPLE MASTECTOMY":\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia associated with calcifications, in one (1) of\u000afifteen (15) blocks (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia.\u000aChanges consistent with prior biopsy site.\u000aNo residual ductal carcinoma in situ identified.\u000aSclerosing adenosis.\u000aFibroadenoma (0.3cm).\u000aCalcifications associated with benign breast epithelium.\u000aSkin with cicatrix.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aC. SPECIMEN LABELED "LEFT BREAST", SIMPLE MASTECTOMY:\u000aBreast tissue with focal epithelial hyperplasia, apocrine metaplasia, columnar\u000aalteration of lobules, and calcifications associated with benign breast\u000aepithelium.\u000aSclerosed intraductal papilloma (0.3cm).\u000aSkin with no significant pathologic change.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aspecimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aPortions of this case were reviewed with ANDREA L. RICHARDSON, M.D., PH.D., who\u000aconcurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: DCIS of right breast potential areas of microinvasion.\u000aOperation: Bilateral breast simple mastectomies with right breast sentinel\u000alymph node biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast carcinoma.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA. Right sentinel lymph nodes (FS).\u000aB. Right breast (short superior, long lateral).\u000aC. Left breast (short superior, long lateral).\u000aO.R. CONSULTATION:\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "1. RIGHT SENTINEL LYMPH NODES" (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aTwo lymph nodes; no tumor present.\u000aOR Consultation by: WILLIAM ROBERT WELCH, M.D.\u000aResident: SAMUEL G KATZ, M.D., PH.D.\u000aThe senior physician certifies that he/she personally conducted a gross and/or\u000amicroscopic examination of the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed\u000athe rapid diagnos(es) related thereto.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Right sentinel lymph nodes", consists of one fragment of\u000ayellow/tan soft tissue (3.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). Two candidate lymph nodes are\u000adissected from the specimen (1.6 cm and REMOVED_DATE cm in maximum dimension,\u000arespectively). The lymph nodes are entirely submitted as REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID remnant, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID remnant, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. Right breast", consists of a 454 gm simple mastectomy\u000aspecimen (17.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with attached nipple areola complex (5.5 x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm) with areola (5.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm) and everted nipple (0.6 x REMOVED_DATE cm in diameter),\u000aand attached separate white/tan skin ellipse (4.0 x REMOVED_DATE cm), which contains a\u000a3.6 cm well-healed surgical scar. The deep margin is composed of unremarkable\u000ayellow/tan fibroadipose tissue and is inked black. The specimen is oriented\u000awith a long suture indicating lateral and a short suture indicating superior.\u000aThere is an area of blue dye (3.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm) directly inferior to the nipple\u000aareola complex and a second area of blue dye (4.0 x REMOVED_DATE cm) surrounding the\u000aseparate skin ellipse. The second are of blue dye extends into a white/tan\u000arubbery fibrous area (5.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) located in the lower outer quadrant\u000a1.6 cm from the deep margin, and is grossly consistent with a prior biopsy\u000asite. The remainder of the breast parenchyma is composed of approximately 20%\u000afibrous tissue the rest is adipose tissue. Approximately 90% of the fibrous\u000aarea and representative sections of the rest of the specimen are submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrous area in lower outer quadrant, REMOVED_DATE frags in each, RSS.\u000aNote: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID contains closest deep margin.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper outer quadrant, fibrous tissue, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper inner quadrant, fibrous tissue, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower inner quadrant, fibrous tissue, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nipple and skin with scar, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aPart C, labeled "#3. Left breast", consists of a 268 gm simple mastectomy\u000aspecimen (17.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with attached nipple areola complex (3.8 x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm) with areola (3.8 x REMOVED_DATE cm) and everted nipple (0.6 cm x REMOVED_DATE cm in\u000adiameter). There is a long suture indicating lateral, and a short suture\u000aindicating superior. The deep margin is composed of grossly unremarkable\u000ayellow-tan fibroadipose tissue and is inked black. There is a white-tan fibrous\u000aarea (2.6 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) located in the upper outer quadrant REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000adeep margin. The remainder of the breast parenchyma is composed of\u000aapproximately 20% fibrous tissue and the rest adipose tissue. 2 candidate\u000aintramammary lymph nodes are identified, each measuring REMOVED_DATE cm in maximum\u000adimension. The entire fibrous area and representative sections of the remainder\u000aof the specimen are submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrous area in upper outer quadrant, 1 to 2 frags in each, ESS\u000a[Note: closest deep margin contained in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID]\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 2 candidate lymph nodes, 2 frags, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower outer quadrant, 2 frags, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper inner quadrant, 2 frags, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower inner quadrant, 2 frags, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nipple, 2 frags, ESS\u000aJDR/abjpMMM\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aMicroscopic/Diagnostic Dictation by Julie D.R. Reimann M.D., Ph.D., on\u000a1/13/2005\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Lucian Chirieac M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
