VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Astrid Weins, M.D., Ph.D.; Eleanor Y. Chen, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Jane E. Brock, M.B., B.S., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZATION EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aSEVERELY ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA bordering on DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU,\u000awith associated calcifications is present in REMOVED_DATE blocks (see NOTE).\u000aArea of scarring and fibrosis suggestive of a prior biopsy site.\u000aCalcifications associated with columnar cell change, cysts and benign\u000aepithelium.\u000aFibroadenomatoid change.\u000aSkeletal muscle is present.\u000aNote: The atypical proliferation shows low to intermediate nuclear grade, has a\u000amicropapillary and flat pattern and is mucin producing. The atypical\u000aproliferation is multifocally < REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior, posterior, inferior,\u000amedial and REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral margin. This case has also been reviewed by\u000aDr. Susan Lester. Evaluation with excision is recommended to exclude diagnostic\u000afeatures of DCIS.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 33-year-old premenopausal woman with L breast mammo on REMOVED_DATE \u000arevealing increase in REMOVED_DATE mm cluster of punctate and\u000aOperation: L breast wire-loc bx..\u000aOperative Findings: None specified.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: None specified.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) L breast loc bx; short stitch - superior, long - lateral and whip stitch\u000achest wall margin --> mammo --> path.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Calcs and wire in specimen. Called OR, Dr. Rhei at 12:29 PM by Dr.\u000aM. Gerlach.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in one part, labeled with the patient's name,\u000aunit number, and "L breast", and consists of an irregular fragment of\u000afibroadipose tissue (3.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a needle-localization wire\u000aentering the anterior aspect of the specimen with no definitive exit, and an\u000aaccompanying radiograph demonstrating multiple punctate calcifications. There\u000ais a short stitch designating superior, long stitch designating lateral, and a\u000awhipstitch designating the chest wall margin per surgeon. The specimen is\u000ainked by the surgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/ orange. The specimen is serially sectioned from superior to inferior\u000aand unremarkable fibrofatty cut surface with approximately 95% white fibrous\u000atissue, to 5% yellow lobulated adipose tissue. The specimen is submitted in\u000aits entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular superior margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Breast sequentially submitted from superior to inferior to include\u000aarea of needle-localization wire (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), multi frags total, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular inferior margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aJenC/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aWednesday REMOVED_MONTH 10, 2008 at 11:00:49AM\u000a[report_end]
