VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Reduction mamoplasty\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BOHDAN POMAHAC M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-06-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Eric C Huang, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: William R Welch, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. SPECIMEN DESIGNATED "RIGHT BREAST TISSUE" (175g):\u000aBenign breast tissue with extensive fat necrosis, dense fibrosis,\u000achronic inflammation, and foreign body giant cell reaction to\u000aSkin with no significant pathologic change.\u000aB. SPECIMEN DESIGNATED "LEFT BREAST TISSUE" (310kg):\u000aBenign breast tissue with atypical ductal hyperplasia (slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000alevel 2).\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia is present.\u000aMultiple non-necrotizing granulomata are present, involving\u000alobules and interlobular stroma (see NOTE).\u000aColumnar hyperplasia.\u000aSkin with no significant pathologic change.\u000aNOTE:\u000aThe differential diagnosis for the granulomatous process includes granulomatous\u000alobular mastitis. However, the finding of granulomata in interlobular stroma,\u000aand the presence of asteroid bodies, raises the possibility of sarcoid\u000agranulomatosis. Clinical correlation is needed.\u000aFungal (MSS) and acid-fast stains are negative for organisms.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Right s/p breast cancer. S/p tram.\u000aOperation: Revision tram, (R) breast (L) breast fat necrosis reduction\u000amammoplasty.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: None given.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right breast tissue - 175g\u000aB/2. Left breast tissue - 310kg\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patients\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "Right breast tissue - 175g", consists of multiple irregular\u000afragments of fibrofatty tissue and tan skin weighing 175 gms, REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm. Sectioning shows dark yellow parenchyma with a small amount of\u000ainterspersed white fibrous tissue. There are multiple areas of fat necrosis\u000abut a mass lesion is not seen. Representative sections are submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: skin and breast tissue, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: breast tissue, 4 frags, RSS.\u000aPart B, labeled "Left breast tissue", consists of multiple irregular fragments\u000aof fibrofatty tissue and dark tan skin weighing 310g, REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm.\u000aSectioning shows predominately fatty cut surface with scant interspersed white\u000afibrous tissue. No lesions are seen. Representative sections are submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: skin and breast tissue, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: breast tissue, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aWP/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Eric C Huang M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by William R Welch M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
