VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Cheryl Adackapara, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, MRI GUIDED WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A REMOVED_DATE CM AREA OF\u000aENHANCMENT:\u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA is present as multiple foci, best seen in blocks\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID (see note).\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU is present (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; levels examined).\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies confirm that the LCIS is positive for estrogen and\u000aprogesterone receptors and negative for REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu and e-cadherin.\u000aNOTE: The ADH has a variety of histologic appearances. Areas in blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID appear to be present in a pagetoid pattern. The atypical cells are estrogen\u000areceptor positive, e-cadherin positive, and negative for p63 and smooth muscle\u000aactin. The cells also show patchy immunoreactivity for cytokeratin REMOVED_DATE \u000aAdditional patterns consist of small foci with cribriform or solid architecture\u000ain blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID (levels examined). These foci are estrogen and\u000aprogesterone receptor positive, e-cadherin positive, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu negative.\u000aDue to the small size of the foci (0.1 to REMOVED_DATE cm) and the variety of histologic\u000aappearances, these lesions are best classified as atypical ductal hyperplasia.\u000aThe entire specimen was examined microscopically, including margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 40 year old premenopausal woman with a family history of breast cancer\u000ain the mother twice (38, 57). REMOVED_DATE  MRI demonstrated a R breast 6 mm area of\u000arapid enhancement for which biopsy was recommended - not amenable to core\u000abiopsy.\u000aOperation: R breast MRI guided wire localized biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal R breast MRI.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) R breast mass - whip stitch -> chest wall margin, short suture ->\u000asuperior, long suture -> lateral.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aSpecimen - wire and vague tissue density (MR loc). OR called. Dr. Sughra\u000aRaza.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "right breast mass with wire", and consists of a REMOVED_DATE (superior to inferior)\u000ax REMOVED_DATE (medial to lateral) x REMOVED_DATE (anterior to posterior) cm breast biopsy with a\u000alocalization wire in place and a short suture marking superior and a long\u000asuture marking the lateral margin. An accompanying specimen radiograph\u000ademonstrates a wire and a vague tissue density. The wire is loosely attached\u000aalong the inferior aspect of the specimen. The specimen is inked by the\u000asurgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange.\u000aThe specimen is sectioned from superior to inferior and displays diffuse firm\u000atan/white dense fibrous tissue occupying up to 99% of the specimen. The\u000aspecimen is entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular sections of the superior margin, 6 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Body submitted from superior to inferior to include paired\u000asections REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, multi frags total, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular sections of inferior margin, 4 frags, ESS.\u000almc/lp\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor\u000acells but only a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in >30% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate immunoreactivity.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on Monday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 21, 2009 at 11:58:17AM\u000a[report_end]
