VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Reduction mamoplasty\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JULIAN J PRIBAZ M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Kevin Golden, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Kirstine Yung-Ting Oh, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA/1). RIGHT BREAST TISSUE, REDUCTION MAMMOPLASTY (690 grams):\u000aBenign breast tissue.\u000aSkin with no significant pathologic change.\u000aB/2). LEFT BREAST TISSUE, REDUCTION MAMMOPLASTY (440 grams):\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU, present as a single focus (slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aUsual ductal hyperplasia.\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium.\u000aSkin with no significant pathologic change.\u000aThis portion of the case was also reviewed by Dr. Andrea Richardson,\u000aBreast Pathology Service, who concurs. Dr. J. Pribaz was informed of\u000athe diagnosis on REMOVED_DATE  at 2:40pm.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: None given.\u000aOperation: Bil breast reduction, abdominal plasty.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Macromastia, abdominal fat pads.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) right breast tissue, 690.\u000aB/2) left breast tissue, 440.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThis case is received in two parts, each labeled with the patient's name and\u000amedical record number.\u000aPart A, "#.1 Right breast tissue", consists of a 690 gram of fibrofatty breast\u000atissue and attached strips of skin in fourteen segments ranging from REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm to REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. The largest segment has several strips of\u000aattached skin, the largest of which measures REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension.\u000aTwo segments of skin are received in separate fragments ranging up to REMOVED_DATE cm\u000ain greatest dimension. Surface of the skin is tan to light brown and\u000aunremarkable except for scattered striae. The breast tissue is composed of\u000asoft yellow fat with large lobules interspersed with streaks of glistening\u000awhite fibrous parenchyma (approximately 40%). There is no evidence of\u000ainduration or nodularity.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fat, breast tissue and skin, 3 frags, RSS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Left breast tissue", consists of a 440 gram of yellow lobulated\u000afibrofatty breast tissue and skin received in ten segments ranging from REMOVED_DATE x\u000a2.0 x REMOVED_DATE cm to REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. The largest segment of the skin attached\u000ato beast tissue is REMOVED_DATE cm and a separate segment measures REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest\u000adimension. Skin surfaces are tan to light brown and unremarkable. The breast\u000atissue is composed of soft yellow and large lobules admixed with glistening\u000awith fibrous parenchyma (and estimated 40% of specimen volume). There is no\u000aevidence of induration or nodularity.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fat, breast tissue and skin, 3 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Additionally submitted breast tissue, 2 frags each, RSS.\u000adf/lp\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Kirstine Yung-Ting Oh M.D., Electronically signed on\u000a11/27/2007\u000a[report_end]
