VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JAMES D IGLEHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Oname O. Burlingame, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Lucian R Chirieac, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. SPECIMEN DESIGNATED "LEFT BREAST BIOPSY AFTER NEEDLE LOCALIZATION":\u000aBenign breast tissue.\u000aColumnar cell changes.\u000aCalcifications associated with benign breast epithelium.\u000aBiopsy site changes.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aB. SPECIMEN DESIGNATED "RIGHT BREAST BIOPSY AFTER NEEDLE LOCALIZATION":\u000aBenign breast tissue.\u000aColumnar cell changes.\u000aApocrine cysts.\u000aPapilloma.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aC. SPECIMEN DESIGNATED "ADDITIONAL TISSUE RIGHT BREAST":\u000aDuctal epithelial hyperplasia.\u000aSclerosing adenosis.\u000aApocrine cysts.\u000aCalcifications in benign breast epithelium.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aNOTE: Dr. William Bennett, Breast Pathology Service, has reviewed select slides\u000afrom this case and concurs with the diagnosis of epithelial hyperplasia.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 49 year old female, bilat breast atypical hyperplasia.\u000aOperation: Bilat wire localized needle biopsies.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Bilat atypical hyperplasia.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) left breast bx after needle loc, short stitch/superior, long\u000astitch/lateral (mammo).\u000aB/2) right breast bx after needle loc, short stitch/superior, long\u000astitch/lateral (mammo).\u000aC/3) additional tissue R breast.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aSpecimen #1: L specimen - calcifications and clip contained in specimen.\u000aCalled to OR, Dr. Iglehart by Dr. MacLean @ 3:40 p.m.\u000aSpecimen #3: 3 specimens received from OR, hookwire included in specimen, no\u000aclip is seen. Called to Dr. Iglehart in OR. S MacLean/Yeh.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, unit number.\u000aPart A, "#1. Left breast Bx after needle loc, short stitch superior, long\u000astitch lateral", consists of an irregular fragment of yellow/tan to pink/tan\u000afibroadipose tissue (3.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which is oriented per surgeon with\u000atwo sutures as indicated. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the\u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. There is one needle\u000alocalization wire present entering the lateral aspect of the specimen and\u000aexiting the medial aspect of the specimen. The specimen was previously\u000aradiographed, confirming the presence of calcifications and a clip within the\u000aspecimen. The specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral to exhibit\u000aapproximately 90% diffusely dense focally cystic pink/white fibrous tissue and\u000a10% yellow/tan adipose tissue. The fibrous tissue extends to all six inked\u000amargins. No discrete masses or lesions are identified. A metal clip is\u000aidentified within the dense fibrous stroma in the lateral aspect of the\u000aspecimen, adjacent to the needle localization wire. The area of possible\u000acalcification identified within the radiograph corresponds to the medial aspect\u000aof the specimen. The specimen is entirely sequentially submitted from medial\u000ato lateral.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Central specimen (probable calcifications in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, clip site in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID),\u000a1 frag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Right breast Bx after needle loc", consists of an unoriented\u000afragment of yellow/tan to pink/tan fibroadipose tissue (2.6 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm)\u000awith one needle localization wire loosely embedded. The specimen is inked\u000ablack per prosector. The specimen was previously radiographed indicating the\u000apresence of an intact hookwire within the specimen. A clip is not identified.\u000aThe specimen is serially sectioned to exhibit approximately 90% diffusely dense\u000apink/tan fibrous tissue with focal areas of hemorrhage and less than 10%\u000ayellow/tan adipose tissue. No discrete masses or lesions are grossly\u000aidentified. The needle localization wire tip is embedded within the fibrous\u000atissue in one aspect of the specimen. The specimen is entirely sequentially\u000asubmitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Specimen sequentially submitted with perpendicular margins in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aand REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and a slice of wire tip in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, multi frags total, ESS.\u000aPlease note the specimen is received without the presence of sutures and is\u000aunoriented. Although, suture orientation listed for part B in the form 86, the\u000acorresponding specimen container is labeled as indicated in the gross\u000adescription with the previous suture designating crossed out. The specimen\u000acontainer is saved.\u000aPart C, "#3. Additional tissue R breast", consists of two unoriented fragments\u000aof yellow/tan to pink/tan fibroadipose tissue (fragment #1 REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm,\u000afragment #2 REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). No needle localization wires are present\u000awithin the fragments of specimen container. Each fragment is inked black. The\u000afragments were previously radiographed indicating that a clip is not present\u000awithin the tissue. Each fragment is serially sectioned, exhibiting\u000aapproximately 85% dense focally cystic pink/white fibrous stroma and 15%\u000ayellow/tan adipose tissue. No discrete masses or lesions are grossly\u000aidentified. Each of the fragments is sequentially entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fragment #1 serially sectioned (perpendicular margins in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), multi frags total, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fragment #2 with perpendicular margins in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, multi frags\u000atotal, ESS.\u000alm/lp\u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-07-REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Lucian R Chirieac M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
