VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ROGER L CHRISTIAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Matthew D. Stachler, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Gilbert Brodsky, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT BREAST, EXCISION WIRE LOCALIZATION FOR CALCIFICATIONS\u000aAND CLIPS:\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aINVASIVE CARCINOMA - Absent\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU - Present\u000aTypes - Micropapillary\u000aNuclear grade - Low\u000aCalcifications in DCIS - Present\u000aCentral necrosis in DCIS - Absent\u000aMicroinvasion - Absent\u000aExtent of DCIS\u000aEstimated overall size - REMOVED_DATE cm\u000aNumber of blocks/total - 3:8 blocks\u000aMargins (DCIS)\u000aNegative (> REMOVED_DATE cm) - All\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU - Absent\u000aOTHER FINDINGS\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia\u000aCore biopsy site\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER, PR, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) - Pending\u000aNOTE: Dr. J. Brock, Breast Pathology Service, has reviewed the case and concurs\u000awith the diagnosis.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: ADH, Lt breast.\u000aOperation: Wire-directed excision.\u000aOperative Findings: See op note.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: None provided.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Tissue left breast; suture - long lateral, short - superior (Bioptics)\u000a(to Pathology and place in refrigerator).\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh and labeled with the patient's name, unit\u000anumber, and "#1. Tissue left breast, suture long lateral, short superior", and\u000aconsists of a REMOVED_DATE gram lumpectomy (5.3 (medial to lateral) x REMOVED_DATE (anterior to\u000aposterior) x REMOVED_DATE (superior to inferior) cm). The specimen is oriented by the\u000asurgeon as long stitch equals lateral and short equals superior. The specimen\u000ais inked by the surgeon per protocol as superior equals red, inferior equals\u000agreen, anterior equals blue, posterior equals black, medial equals orange, and\u000alateral equals yellow. There are two localization wires with the first\u000aentering the anterior/lateral side and exiting the medial side and the second\u000awire enters the lateral side. Accompanying radiograph (viewed via centricity)\u000ademonstrates positioning of the wires with the anterior wire anterior to the\u000acalcifications and the posterior wire between the two (of 3) more posterior\u000aclips. On serial sectioning of the specimen, two biopsy cavities near the\u000amid/upper thick portion of one of the wires were identified, both of these\u000acontained metal clips. A third biopsy cavity was found near the\u000amedial/posterior border near the tip of the other wire which also contained a\u000aclip. There was a tan, slightly firm fibrous area (1.8 (medial to lateral) x\u000a1.2 (anterior to posterior) x REMOVED_DATE (superior to inferior) cm), which included\u000athe calcification seen on radiograph. This area was REMOVED_DATE cm to the superior\u000amargin, REMOVED_DATE cm to the inferior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm to the anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm to\u000athe posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm to the medial margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm to the lateral\u000amargin. The remainder of the specimen was unremarkable with the total specimen\u000acomprised of approximately 60 percent adipose tissue and 40% fibrous tissue.\u000aThe specimen was entirely submitted for histological analysis to include the\u000abiopsy cavities in blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID. The firm area is submitted in\u000ablocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin, perpendicular, 5 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Serial sectioning of the specimen, biopsy cavities in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, firm area in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, perpendicular, 5 frags and 4 frags\u000arespectively, ESS.\u000aTAC/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Gilbert Brodsky M.D., Electronically signed on Tuesday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 01, 2009 at 05:29:07PM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM:\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for DCIS (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not\u000ayet been determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only\u000aa portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a< 30% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>30% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aAddendum #1 by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aThursday REMOVED_MONTH 03, 2009 at 01:25:36PM\u000a[report_end]
