VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: DIANE C LOCKHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Kristin S. Ricci, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Deborah A Dillon, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS AND A CLIP:\u000aSeverely ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, with associated calcifications, is\u000apresent (see note).\u000aATYPICAL LOBULAR HYPERPLASIA is present.\u000aApocrine cysts and columnar cell change with associated calcifications.\u000aRadial sclerosing lesion (0.4 cm).\u000aBiopsy site changes are present.\u000aNOTE: A highly atypical intraductal proliferation is present on slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID,\u000ameasuring REMOVED_DATE cm. The differential diagnosis includes a small focus of\u000alow-grade DCIS versus atypical ductal hyperplasia. Based on multifocal\u000aretention of a peripheral columnar cell population, a diagnosis of atypical\u000aductal hyperplasia is favored. Slides also seen by Dr. Susan Lester and Dr.\u000aAndrea Richardson, who concur.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 50-year-old female with microcalcifications, left breast, on screening\u000amammogram. Status post stereotactic core biopsy (8/11/08) (positive) ADH.\u000aRecommended for surgical incision.\u000aOperation: Left needle localization breast biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast ADH.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast tissue, short superior, long lateral - mammo.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aClip, wire and residual calcifications are within the specimen.\u000aSignature: Dr. Gombos\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "#1. L breast tissue, short superior, long lateral", and consists of a REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm excision of fibroadipose tissue, oriented by sutures designating\u000asuperior (short suture) and lateral (long suture) margins. The specimen is\u000ainked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange\u000a(inked by surgeon). The specimen is received with an accompanying radiograph\u000ademonstrating a localizing wire in place and area of a metallic surgical clip\u000acentrally within the specimen. Please note: There is no localizing wire in\u000aplace upon specimen's arrival to pathology. The specimen is sectioned (roughly\u000afrom anterior to posterior), revealing tan/white fibrous tissue (30 to 40% of\u000athe specimen), admixed with relatively unremarkable soft, lobulated adipose\u000atissue. Fibrous tissue is granular but otherwise relatively unremarkable.\u000aThere is a REMOVED_DATE cm cavity filled with a yellow/tan, foreign material and\u000acontaining a metallic surgical clip (slice #6-#8), REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest\u000alateral margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin,\u000a1.0 cm from the inferior margin, and at least REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior and\u000aposterior margins. There are no definitive additional abnormalities noted\u000agrossly. The specimen is entirely sequentially submitted, with perpendicular\u000aanterior margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, perpendicular posterior margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, and area of\u000ainterest in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Multi frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000adb/elw\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
