VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Consult material\u000aProcedure Date:\u000aOrdering Provider: JAMES D IGLEHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Stephanie Schulte, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Jane E. Brock, M.B., B.S., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aCONSULT SLIDES FROM NEWTON-WELLESLY HOSPITAL, DEPARTMENT OF PATHOLOGY, NEWTON,\u000aMA\u000aRIGHT BREAST, MEDIAL, CORE NEEDLE BIOPSIES FOR MICROCALCIFICATIONS (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID;\u000a12/17/08):\u000aATYPICAL LOBULAR HYPERPLASIA with pagetoid spread, involving an area of\u000aadenosis and an area of apocrine cysts with papillary architecture (see Note).\u000aMicrocalcifications are associated with apocrine cysts and focally with\u000acolumnar cell change WITH ATYPIA (see Note)\u000aImmunohistochemical stains for calponin, muscle actin and smooth muscle myosin\u000areviewed at BWH highlight intact myoepithelial cells in all lobules.\u000aAn E-cadherin stain reviewed highlights absent membranous staining in atypical\u000alobular cells (block 1).\u000aNOTE: The ALH appears to be an incidental finding. Calcifications are\u000apredominantly associated with apocrine cysts (some with papillary\u000aarchitecture), including those involved by ALH. A small area of columnar cell\u000achange with calcifications (blocks 2 and 3) adjacent to the apocrine cysts\u000ashows focal nuclear and cytological atypia (flat and micropapillary atypia).\u000aExcision for definitive classification is recommended.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Grouped microcalcifications in the right medial breast on baseline\u000amammogram REMOVED_DATE  positive family history of breast cancer including mother -\u000a?fibrocystic changes vs. DCIS\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Fibrocystic changes w/ductal hyperplasia, florid sclerosing\u000aadenosis with calcifications; small intraductal papilloma\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aConsult slides.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aReceived from Newton-Wellesley Hospital, Pathology Department, 2014 Washington\u000aStreet, Newton, MA REMOVED_CASE_ID, are nineteen (19) glass slides.\u000aThe slides are labeled "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" and sublabeled "1 REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" through "1 3L", "1\u000aCALPONIN", "1 E-CADHERIN", "1 ACTIN", "1 MYOSIN", "2 1L" through "2 3L", "3 1L"\u000athrough "3 3L", "3 ACTIN", "3 CALPONIN", "3 MYOSIN" and "4 1L" through "4 3L",\u000acorresponding to a right breast, medial, stereotactic needle biopsies 1) (0.3 x\u000a1.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm) 2) (0.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) REMOVED_DATE (2 x 2 x REMOVED_DATE cm, and REMOVED_DATE cm)\u000aperformed on REMOVED_DATE  according to the accompanying pathology report bearing\u000athe patient's name.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aTuesday February 03, 2009 at 09:57:31AM\u000a[report_end]
