VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: J. GREGORY KANE M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Christian J. Davidson, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR TUBULAR DENSITIES:\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU is present in one block (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). An\u000aimmunohistochemical study confirms the absence of e-cadherin.\u000aIntraductal papillomas with florid epithelial hyperplasia and focal\u000ainfarction and hemorrhage, involving the entire specimen.\u000aNote: The extensive involvement of ducts by papillomas would correlate\u000awith the patient's bloody nipple discharge. No invasive carcinoma or\u000aductal carcinoma in situ are seen after complete pathologic evaluation of\u000athe specimen, including margins.\u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. Deborah Dillon, who concurs with this\u000ainterpretation.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR TUBULAR DENSITIES, MEDIAL TO THE\u000aFIRST SPECIMEN:\u000aIntraductal papillomas with florid epithelial hyperplasia and focal\u000ainfarction and hemorrhage, involving the entire specimen.\u000aNote: No invasive carcinoma or ductal carcinoma in situ are seen after\u000acomplete pathologic evaluation of the specimen, including margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Bloody nipple discharge on right with suspicious mammogram finding in\u000alower breast with dilated duct.\u000aOperation: Right beast resection (wire loc).\u000aOperative Findings: Dilated duct and surrounding reaction.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Duct ectasia and ? DCIS.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right breast tissue (white vicryl tie on dilated duct, short superior,\u000along lateral black silk tie).\u000aB/2. Right breast tissue medial to specimen 1.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Distal wire (no hook) and tubular densities in sample #1.\u000aHook wire in tubular densities in sample #2, called to OR 4:05 pm by Dr. H.\u000aPeters.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "right breast tissue", and consists of a fragment of\u000afibroadipose tissue (6.0 cm S-I x REMOVED_DATE cm A-P x REMOVED_DATE cm M-L) with a short suture\u000adesignating superior, long suture lateral, and a Vicryl tie designating a\u000adilated duct. The specimen is received with a loosely adherent needle\u000alocalization wire running from the medial aspect of the specimen. The dilated\u000aduct (1.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm) is located at the medial aspect of the specimen. The\u000aspecimen is received with a radiograph showing multiple lobulated and\u000aill-defined densities throughout the specimen. The specimen is inked by the\u000asurgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange.\u000aThe specimen is serially sectioned from superior to inferior to exhibit a\u000atan-white ill-defined focally cystic/hemorrhagic nodular area (3.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm) located REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior margin,\u000a0.3 cm from the medial margin, abutting the lateral margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000aanterior margin and REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin. The remaining breast\u000aparenchyma is comprised of 95% lobulated adipose tissue and 5% fibrous tissue.\u000aThe area designated as the dilated duct, on cut section, is smooth and\u000awhite-brown. The specimen is submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular superior margin, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Body, sequentially submitted from superior to inferior to include\u000aarea of dilated duct (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) and irregular firm area (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), multi frags\u000atotal, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular inferior margin, multi frags total, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "right breast tissue medial to specimen #1", and consists of an\u000aunoriented fragment of fibroadipose tissue (5.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The specimen\u000aradiograph shows tubular densities. The fragment of soft tissue was entirely\u000ainked black, although due its disrupted nature, the inked surface may not\u000arepresent true margin. The specimen is serially sectioned to exhibit a grossly\u000aunremarkable fibrofatty cut surface comprised of 20% white fibrous tissue to\u000a80% yellow lobulated adipose tissue. The specimen was submitted in its\u000aentirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Multi frags total, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000ajc/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aWednesday REMOVED_MONTH 15, 2009 at 04:38:37PM\u000a[report_end]
