L, REMOVED_DATE L, REMOVED_DATE L, REMOVED_DATE L, REMOVED_DATE L) MJR/dg CO Notes: (Continued) Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 1130 PAT.GILD REMOVED_DATE  - 1103 PAT.MURJ REMOVED_DATE  - 1105 PAT.MURJ REMOVED_DATE  - 0912 PAT.MURJ REMOVED_DATE  - 1010 PAT.DORD REMOVED_DATE  - 1001 PAT.MURJ ADDENDUM REPORTAddendum #1 Entered: REMOVED_DATE -1304 *** ADDITIONAL DIAGNOSTIC INFORMATION *** This report contains an addendum to the original for the purpose of providing additional diagnostic information. The original diagnosis is unchanged. This case has been reviewed by Dr. Frederick Koerner, of the Massachusetts General Hospital, who concurs with the diagnosis. He comments: "I agree wholeheartedly with your analysis of this case. The sections of this core biopsy specimen show a background of slight fibrocystic changes. A population of atypical ductal cells occupies the acini of several terminal duct-lobular units in block "1". The cells appear enlarged and they possess slightly enlarged nuclei, homogeneous chromatin, inconspicuous nucleoli, and modest amounts of eosinophilic, apical cytoplasm. The cells grow in a single, crowded layer; they do not show genuine stratification nor do they form cribriform spaces, trabecular bars, or Roman bridges. Like you, I would classify the proliferation as atypical ductal hyperplasia of columnar cell type. I suspect that the atypical cells represent a neoplastic proliferation, but I cannot exclude the possibility that they constitute an unusual form of ductal hyperplasia. An excision of this region of the patient's left breast would probably clarify this uncertainty."Addendum Final: ROSS,MICHAEL J. M.D. * REMOVED_DATE  <04/04/06-1407> by MD.ROSMJ Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 1304 PAT.MURJCODES: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - Left breast, NO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - Left breast, NO-Atypical REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - Columnar epithe REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - Columnar epithe-Lesion, NOS REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - Columnar epithe-Atypical hyperp REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID 