VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID:REMOVED_CASE_ID Report Status: Signed\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aDIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. Left breast, excisional biopsy:\u000aLow grade phyllodes tumor REMOVED_DATE cm. (See note).\u000aFibrocystic changes.\u000aSmall intraductal papilloma.\u000aB. Left breast, excisional biopsy:\u000aFibrocystic changes, adenosis and ductal hyperplasia, see note.\u000aFibroadenoma REMOVED_DATE cm. with ductal hyperplasia.\u000aNOTE: In specimen A, the phyllodes tumor is present at the inked specimen\u000aedges. The entire case was sent to Massachusetts General Hospital to\u000aDr. Melinda Fan Lerwill who concurs with our diagnosis. She adds:\u000a"I would classify the mass in part A as a low grade phyllodes tumor.\u000aThe primary features that help to distinguish it from a fibroadenoma are\u000aits morphologic heterogeneity and the presence of irregular, leaf-like\u000afronds. The morphologic heterogeneity is manifest in several ways.\u000aThere is variation in the stromal cellularity, from regions that are\u000aessentially acellular and hyalinized to regions that are moderately\u000ahypercellular. There is variation in the distribution of glands to\u000astroma, with some areas demonstrating more pronounced expansion of the\u000astromal compartment. The glands are also heterogeneous with irregular\u000adilated contours. All these features contrast with the uniform\u000acellularity, consistent distribution of glands to stroma, and regular\u000aintracanalicular or pericanalicular glands typically seen in\u000afibroadenomas. I also found up to 4 stromal mitotic figures per 10 high\u000apower fields in one of the more cellular regions of the tumor, which\u000awould be a higher rate that expected for a fibroadenoma. There are a\u000afew areas where there do appear to be genuine fronds projecting into\u000adilated ducts and/or cystic spaces, a features that I consider a very\u000astrong indicator of the diagnosis of phyllodes tumor. Stromal\u000aovergrowth is absent, and the tumor extends to the margins of the\u000aexcision. At our institution, we generally do not make a diagnosis of\u000abenign phyllodes tumor, since even tumors with "benign" histologic\u000afeatures can recur and in extremely rare instances metastasize. We\u000aprefer instead a low, intermediate and high grade classification scheme\u000afor these neoplasms.\u000aThe epithelial proliferations in slides REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID appear to be usual-\u000atype ductal hyperplasia. The cells are overlapping, show maturation,\u000ahave nuclear grooves and lack polarization, all features that support an\u000ainterpretation of usual-type rather than atypical ductal hyperplasia.\u000aSimilarly, I would also characterize the epithelial proliferation in\u000apart B as florid usual-type ductal hyperplasia. Although the cells are\u000alittle larger in slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, they are still overlapping, have nuclear\u000agrooves, and lack polarization. They do not demonstrate the monotonous\u000ahyperchromatic morphology of cells typically seen in low grade atypia."\u000aFinal Diagnosis by: Nicole A. Mihok, M.D.\u000aClinical diagnosis & history:\u000aLeft breast mass\u000aTissue-Designation:\u000aA.Breast, excision with margins - left\u000aB.Breast, excision with margins - left\u000aGross description:\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin in two containers.\u000aSpecimen A labeled "left index fibroadenoma", designated "breast" on the jar\u000aand consists of an unoriented REMOVED_DATE gram, bosselated, oval shaped, white tissue\u000athat is up to REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. It is not oriented. The margins are inked\u000ablack. The cut section shows rubbery, white parenchyma with a slightly bulging\u000acut surface that measures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. The entire specimen is submitted\u000ain cassettes REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID. 18 agg. all.\u000aSpecimen B labeled "left breast, ? fibroadenoma" consists of an unoriented REMOVED_DATE \u000agram, REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. portion of tissue received in formalin. It is not\u000aoriented. The margins are inked black. The specimen is serially sectioned and\u000ashows a variegated, tan-yellow portion of tissue. The entire specimen is\u000asubmitted from one end towards the other in cassettes REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID. 6 agg. all.\u000aDictated by: NAM____(signature on file)____RICHARD SAMPSON, M.D. REMOVED_DATE  (Final)\u000a____(signature on file)____NICOLE MIHOK, M.D. REMOVED_DATE  (Final)\u000a[report_end]
